---
title: Providers
id: providers
order: 3
description: "Pick and configure where a TanStack AI sandbox runs (local process, Docker container, Docker Sandboxes microVM, Daytona, Vercel, Upstash Box, Blaxel, E2B, boxd, or Railway) and what each one can do."
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A provider owns the isolation primitive: where the harness actually runs. Every\nprovider implements the same "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SandboxProvider"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SandboxHandle"},{"type":"text","value":" contract, so the\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./workspace","children":[{"type":"text","value":"workspace"}]},{"type":"text","value":" you hand the agent and the "},{"type":"link","href":"./policy","children":[{"type":"text","value":"policy"}]},{"type":"text","value":" that guards\nit are provider-agnostic. Pick a provider for the isolation, auth, and\nsnapshot/resume behaviour you need; the rest of your sandbox definition stays the\nsame."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Provider-native snapshots and resume keep or recreate provider state. They can\nreduce bootstrap time. "},{"type":"link","href":"./portable-snapshots","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Portable Snapshots"}]},{"type":"text","value":" store\ncompleted workspace data in your application persistence for reconstruction."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The provider is "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"where"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the agent runs. For "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"which"}]},{"type":"text","value":" agent runs (Grok Build,\nClaude Code, Codex, OpenCode, or any ACP agent via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"acpCompatible"},{"type":"text","value":") see\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./harnesses","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Harnesses"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"choosing-a-provider","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choosing a provider"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Provider"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Package"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Isolation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Notes"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Local process"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-sandbox-local-process"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"none (host)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The fast, no-Docker dev loop. Trusted/dev use only."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Docker"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"container"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Real isolation; commit-based snapshots, fork, resume-by-id."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Docker Sandboxes ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sbx"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"microVM"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Native Docker Sandboxes. Same package as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dockerSandbox()"},{"type":"text","value":", different export: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sbxSandbox()"},{"type":"text","value":". Needs the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sbx"},{"type":"text","value":" CLI, a login, and a hypervisor."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Daytona"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-sandbox-daytona"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"cloud sandbox"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Managed "},{"type":"link","href":"https://www.daytona.io/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Daytona"}]},{"type":"text","value":" sandboxes; snapshots after setup, port preview links, resume-by-id. Needs "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DAYTONA_API_KEY"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-sandbox-vercel"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"microVM"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Managed "},{"type":"link","href":"https://vercel.com/docs/sandbox","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel Sandbox"}]},{"type":"text","value":" microVMs; exposed-port domains, resume-by-id (persistent). Needs "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"VERCEL_TOKEN"},{"type":"text","value":" + team/project."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sprites"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-sandbox-sprites"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"stateful sandbox"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Managed "},{"type":"link","href":"https://sprites.dev","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sprites"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (Fly.io) sandboxes; durable filesystem, in-place checkpoints, single proxied public-URL port, resume-by-id. Needs "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SPRITES_API_KEY"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Upstash Box"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-sandbox-upstash-box"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"cloud sandbox"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Managed "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/upstash/box","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Upstash Box"}]},{"type":"text","value":" sandboxes; interactive processes over a WebSocket session (real pid, stdin, signals), native snapshots, preview URLs, resume-by-id. Needs "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"UPSTASH_BOX_API_KEY"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Blaxel"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-sandbox-blaxel"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"cloud sandbox"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Managed "},{"type":"link","href":"https://blaxel.ai","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Blaxel"}]},{"type":"text","value":" sandboxes; durable filesystem, per-port preview URLs, native file watch, resume-by-id. Snapshot/fork remain disabled while the source-scoped private-preview semantics are unproven. Accepts "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BL_API_KEY"},{"type":"text","value":" + "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BL_WORKSPACE"},{"type":"text","value":" or SDK-resolved CLI or client credentials."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"E2B"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-sandbox-e2b"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"microVM"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Managed "},{"type":"link","href":"https://e2b.dev","children":[{"type":"text","value":"E2B"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Firecracker sandboxes. Native snapshots and fork, preview URLs, writable stdin, process-group kill, resume-by-id (also wakes a paused sandbox). Needs "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"E2B_API_KEY"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"boxd"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-sandbox-boxd"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"microVM"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Managed "},{"type":"link","href":"https://boxd.sh","children":[{"type":"text","value":"boxd"}]},{"type":"text","value":" KVM microVMs; live fork (memory and processes), snapshots that restore into a new machine, persistent disk, resume-by-id across stop, suspend and hibernate, one public HTTPS URL per machine. Needs "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BOXD_API_KEY"},{"type":"text","value":" + "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BOXD_ORG"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Railway"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-sandbox-railway"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"microVM"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Managed "},{"type":"link","href":"https://railway.com","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Railway"}]},{"type":"text","value":" sandboxes. Private networking to your other Railway services, live disk fork, environment-scoped checkpoints that survive deletion of their source, durable exec sessions, create-time HTTP domains, resume-by-id. Needs "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RAILWAY_API_TOKEN"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RAILWAY_TOKEN"},{"type":"text","value":" + an environment."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Most providers are their own package. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dockerSandbox()"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sbxSandbox()"},{"type":"text","value":" both\ncome from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker"},{"type":"text","value":". The constructor is the only thing that\ndiffers between them:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { localProcessSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-local-process'\nimport { dockerSandbox, sbxSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker'\nimport { daytonaSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-daytona'\nimport { vercelSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-vercel'\nimport { upstashBoxSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-upstash-box'\nimport { blaxelSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-blaxel'\nimport { e2bSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-e2b'\nimport { boxdSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-boxd'\nimport { railwaySandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-railway'\n\nconst dev = localProcessSandbox() // runs on your host\nconst isolated = dockerSandbox({ image: 'node:22' }) // container\nconst microvm = sbxSandbox() // Docker Sandboxes microVM\nconst daytona = daytonaSandbox({ apiKey: process.env.DAYTONA_API_KEY }) // managed cloud sandbox\nconst vercel = vercelSandbox({ runtime: 'node24' }) // managed Vercel microVM\nconst box = upstashBoxSandbox({ apiKey: process.env.UPSTASH_BOX_API_KEY }) // managed Upstash Box\nconst blaxel = blaxelSandbox() // managed Blaxel sandbox; uses API-key or CLI credentials\nconst e2b = e2bSandbox() // managed E2B microVM; reads E2B_API_KEY\nconst boxd = boxdSandbox({ org: 'acme' }) // managed boxd microVM; reads BOXD_API_KEY\nconst railway = railwaySandbox() // managed Railway sandbox; reads RAILWAY_TOKEN + RAILWAY_ENVIRONMENT_ID","lang":"ts"},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cloud providers (including Daytona, Vercel, Sprites, Upstash Box, Blaxel, E2B, boxd, and Railway)\nrun remotely. When you drive them from your laptop, "},{"type":"link","href":"./tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":" bridged\nfrom "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" can't dial your machine's\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"localhost"},{"type":"text","value":", you need the bridge tunnel. See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"tools guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the\nngrok subpath, and the "},{"type":"link","href":"./cloudflare","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cloudflare guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the edge-native\nco-located model."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"local-process","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Local process"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { localProcessSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-local-process'\n\nconst dev = localProcessSandbox()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Isolation:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" none. The harness runs directly on your host, inheriting your\nhost environment. Use it for trusted or dev work only. There is no boundary\nbetween the agent and your machine."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Auth / env:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" inherits the host environment. Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"authMode"},{"type":"text","value":" on the harness\n("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'host'"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'api-key'"},{"type":"text","value":"). The provider does not pick this. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./auth","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Harness Auth"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Snapshot / resume:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" no snapshots and no durable resume-by-id; each run\nre-creates and re-bootstraps under the same identity. The snapshot step is\nskipped silently (see "},{"type":"link","href":"#capabilities","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Capabilities"}]},{"type":"text","value":")."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"host-login-vs-api-key-scrubenv","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Host login vs API key ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scrubEnv"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The provider is where the agent runs, not how it signs in. The default\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"authMode"},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'api-key'"},{"type":"text","value":". Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'host'"},{"type":"text","value":" when the machine already has a CLI\nlogin. A local-process run can be your laptop or a GitHub runner. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./auth","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Harness Auth"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"localProcessSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":" inherits the host environment, including any API keys\nexported there. If you set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"authMode: 'host'"},{"type":"text","value":", pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scrubEnv"},{"type":"text","value":" so those keys\ndo not override the CLI login:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { localProcessSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-local-process'\n\nconst hostLogin = localProcessSandbox({\n  scrubEnv: ['XAI_API_KEY', 'GROK_API_KEY'],\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If the same local-process sandbox runs on a CI machine, set\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"authMode: 'api-key'"},{"type":"text","value":". Then inject the key as a workspace secret. Isolated and\ncloud providers have no host CLI login. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"authMode: 'api-key'"},{"type":"text","value":" and\nworkspace secrets there."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"windows-process-teardown-logger","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Windows process teardown ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"logger"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Killing a spawned process means killing the whole tree, and on Windows that takes\nmore than "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"taskkill /T"},{"type":"text","value":". Commands run through a git-bash "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sh"},{"type":"text","value":", and MSYS's\nfork emulation runs the final command of a statement list, such as the\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tail -f"},{"type":"text","value":" behind a "},{"type":"link","href":"./journal","children":[{"type":"text","value":"journal"}]},{"type":"text","value":" follow read, under an intermediate shell\nthat immediately exits. Windows never reparents, so the surviving process points\nat a dead parent and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"taskkill /T"},{"type":"text","value":", which walks only live parent links, cannot\nreach it "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"while still exiting "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0"}]},{"type":"text","value":". Left alone, every follow read leaks a\nprocess for the life of the machine."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"localProcessSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":" therefore consults MSYS's own process table, which does\nkeep the logical parentage before killing, then kills any descendant "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/T"},{"type":"text","value":" missed.\nTeardown is total by construction: it never throws, because a throwing kill would\nstrand a run mid-flight. That means a kill it genuinely cannot complete (a\nprotected process, access denied) is otherwise invisible, so pass a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"logger"},{"type":"text","value":" to\nsee it:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { localProcessSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-local-process'\n\nconst dev = localProcessSandbox({\n  logger: {\n    warn: (message, meta) => console.warn(message, meta),\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Any object with a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"warn(message, meta?)"},{"type":"text","value":" method works, so the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"InternalLogger"},{"type":"text","value":"\nyour adapter already receives can be handed straight in. A process that had\nalready exited on its own is "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"not"}]},{"type":"text","value":" a failure and is never reported."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Nothing here changes on POSIX, where "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sh"},{"type":"text","value":" really is the command's parent and\nsignalling the wrapper is enough."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"docker","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Docker"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { dockerSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker'\n\nconst isolated = dockerSandbox({ image: 'node:22' })","lang":"ts"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Isolation:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" a real container boundary between the agent and your host."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Auth / env:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" no host login; provide credentials as workspace secrets, which\nare injected into the container env at create/resume. The agent reaches host\ntools over "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"host.docker.internal"},{"type":"text","value":" (see "},{"type":"link","href":"./tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":")."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Snapshot / resume:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" full commit-based snapshots, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fork"},{"type":"text","value":", and resume-by-id.\nBootstrap snapshots after "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setup"},{"type":"text","value":" completes, so subsequent runs resume from the\nsnapshot instead of re-running setup."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"docker-sandboxes-sbx","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Docker Sandboxes (sbx)"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { sbxSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker'\nimport { defineSandbox, defineWorkspace, githubRepo } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n\nconst isolated = sbxSandbox({\n  allowNetwork: ['*.npmjs.org', 'registry.npmjs.org'],\n})\n\nconst sandbox = defineSandbox({\n  id: 'repo-agent',\n  provider: isolated,\n  workspace: defineWorkspace({\n    source: githubRepo({ repo: 'owner/repo' }),\n    setup: ['pnpm install'],\n  }),\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Isolation:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" a hypervisor microVM. The sandbox has its own kernel and its own Docker daemon. This is not "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dockerSandbox()"},{"type":"text","value":", which starts a container."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Needs:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sbx"},{"type":"text","value":" on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PATH"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sbx login"},{"type":"text","value":" (or a PAT piped to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sbx login --password-stdin"},{"type":"text","value":" in CI), and a hypervisor (Hyper-V, Virtualization.framework, or KVM). A Docker socket is not enough."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Workspace:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sbx create --clone"},{"type":"text","value":" copies a host Git repo into the VM. Pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"workspaceDir"},{"type":"text","value":" that contains "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".git"},{"type":"text","value":", or set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"workspace.source"},{"type":"text","value":" to a git URL. If there is no Git repo, create throws. There is no bind-mount fallback."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Auth / env:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" inject API keys as workspace secrets. v1 does not call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sbx secret"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Snapshot / resume:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" no snapshots and no fork. Resume reconnects by name ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sbx ls"},{"type":"text","value":"). A stopped sandbox starts again on the next "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sbx exec"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Network:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" this is the first provider with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"networkPolicy: true"},{"type":"text","value":". See "},{"type":"link","href":"./policy","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Policy"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"daytona","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Daytona"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { daytonaSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-daytona'\n\nconst daytona = daytonaSandbox({\n  apiKey: process.env.DAYTONA_API_KEY,\n  snapshot: 'daytona-medium',\n  autoStopInterval: 0,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Isolation:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" a managed cloud sandbox on a remote VM you do not run yourself."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Auth / env:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" needs "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DAYTONA_API_KEY"},{"type":"text","value":". Put harness credentials in\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./provisioning","children":[{"type":"text","value":"workspace secrets"}]},{"type":"text","value":". At create and snapshot restore,\nDaytona stores each value as an organization Secret and mounts a\nplaceholder in the sandbox env. The create record, the dashboard env\nview, and session command strings do not contain the real value. Daytona\nsubstitutes the value on outbound HTTPS requests. Per-command "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"opts.env"},{"type":"text","value":"\nuses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"executeCommand"},{"type":"text","value":"'s env argument or a sourced env file. It never\nwrites "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"export KEY="},{"type":"text","value":" prefixes into the command string."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Snapshot / resume:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" point-in-time snapshots after setup (default when\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lifecycle.snapshot"},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'after-setup'"},{"type":"text","value":"). Pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"snapshot"},{"type":"text","value":" on\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"daytonaSandbox()"},{"type":"text","value":" to pick the Daytona image (for example\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'daytona-medium'"},{"type":"text","value":"). Resume starts a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stopped"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"archived"},{"type":"text","value":" sandbox, then\nreturns the handle."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Idle stop:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Daytona stops an idle sandbox after 15 minutes by default.\nSet "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"autoStopInterval"},{"type":"text","value":" in minutes to change that. Pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0"},{"type":"text","value":" to turn auto-stop\noff. Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ephemeral: true"},{"type":"text","value":" to delete the sandbox when it stops."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Network:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"policy.capabilities.network: 'deny'"},{"type":"text","value":" blocks all outbound\nnetwork on create."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Working directory:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the portable root "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/workspace"},{"type":"text","value":" maps to\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/home/daytona/workspace"},{"type":"text","value":" by default. Override with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"workdir"},{"type":"text","value":" on\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"daytonaSandbox()"},{"type":"text","value":" if you need another path."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stdin:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" spawned processes accept host stdin ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"writableStdin: true"},{"type":"text","value":")."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Privileges:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the Daytona user is not root. Package installs in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setup"},{"type":"text","value":"\nmust use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sudo -n"},{"type":"text","value":" (for example "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sudo -n apt-get install …"},{"type":"text","value":"). Do not put\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sudo *"},{"type":"text","value":" in a "},{"type":"link","href":"./policy","children":[{"type":"text","value":"policy"}]},{"type":"text","value":" deny list for this provider."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bridge:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the sandbox is remote, so a "},{"type":"link","href":"./tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"bridged tool"}]},{"type":"text","value":" call cannot\nreach your laptop's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"localhost"},{"type":"text","value":". In local dev, tunnel the bridge (see\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":"). A deployed orchestrator is reachable without a tunnel."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default headless path on Daytona:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { grokBuildText } from '@tanstack/ai-grok-build'\nimport {\n  defineSandbox,\n  defineSandboxPolicy,\n  defineWorkspace,\n  gitSkill,\n  githubRepo,\n  withSandbox,\n} from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { daytonaSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-daytona'\n\nconst sandbox = defineSandbox({\n  id: 'daytona-agent',\n  provider: daytonaSandbox({\n    apiKey: process.env.DAYTONA_API_KEY,\n    snapshot: 'daytona-medium',\n  }),\n  workspace: defineWorkspace({\n    source: githubRepo({ repo: 'owner/app' }),\n    skills: [gitSkill({ repo: 'owner/skills-pack' })],\n  }),\n  policy: defineSandboxPolicy({\n    default: 'allow',\n  }),\n})\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: grokBuildText('grok-build'),\n  messages: [{ role: 'user', content: 'List the project files.' }],\n  middleware: [withSandbox(sandbox)],\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Headless Grok Build and Codex stay on auto-approve with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"default: 'allow'"},{"type":"text","value":".\nIsolation is the Daytona VM. Use Claude Code when you need command-level deny."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"vercel","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { vercelSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-vercel'\n\nconst vercel = vercelSandbox({ runtime: 'node24' })","lang":"ts"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Isolation:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" a managed microVM (Vercel Sandbox)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Auth / env:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" needs "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"VERCEL_TOKEN"},{"type":"text","value":" plus a team/project. Harness credentials\nare injected as workspace secrets."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Snapshot / resume:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" persistent resume-by-id with a durable filesystem, plus\nexposed-port domains for previews."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bridge:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" like Daytona, it is a remote VM, so bridged tools need the tunnel in local\ndev (see "},{"type":"link","href":"./tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":")."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"sprites","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sprites"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { spritesSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-sprites'\n\nconst sprites = spritesSandbox({ apiKey: process.env.SPRITES_API_KEY })","lang":"ts"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Isolation:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" a managed "},{"type":"link","href":"https://sprites.dev","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sprites"}]},{"type":"text","value":" stateful sandbox\n(Fly.io), a remote VM you do not run yourself."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Auth / env:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" needs "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SPRITES_API_KEY"},{"type":"text","value":" (token form\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"org/projectNumber/tokenId/secret"},{"type":"text","value":"); override the control-plane URL with\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"apiUrl"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SPRITES_API_URL"},{"type":"text","value":". Harness credentials are injected as workspace\nsecrets."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Snapshot / resume:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" resume-by-id reconnects to the named Sprite (its\nfilesystem is durable across idle suspend/resume). "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"snapshot()"},{"type":"text","value":" creates a\nSprite "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"checkpoint"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (a save point of the writable overlay); restore is\n"},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"in-place"}]},{"type":"text","value":" on the same Sprite via the handle's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"restoreCheckpoint()"},{"type":"text","value":" /\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"listCheckpoints()"},{"type":"text","value":". A checkpoint does not survive Sprite deletion, so the\nprovider intentionally does "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"not"}]},{"type":"text","value":" implement the reconstruct-after-gone\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"restoreSnapshot"},{"type":"text","value":", when a Sprite is gone the framework degrades to a fresh\ncreate instead. Restore restarts the environment and can take minutes;\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"restoreCheckpoint()"},{"type":"text","value":" polls the workspace until it is listable again before\nresolving. Note that immediately after a restore the overlay can be listable\nwhile individual file reads briefly return an I/O error as it settles, so retry\nreads if you act on the filesystem the instant restore returns."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ports:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" a Sprite proxies a single internal HTTP port (default "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"8080"},{"type":"text","value":",\nconfigurable via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"httpPort"},{"type":"text","value":") to its always-on public URL. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ports.connect(8080)"},{"type":"text","value":"\nswitches the URL to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"public"},{"type":"text","value":" auth and returns it; other ports are not exposed."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bridge:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" like Daytona and Vercel, it is a remote VM, so bridged tools need the tunnel in\nlocal dev (see "},{"type":"link","href":"./tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":")."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"upstash-box","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Upstash Box"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { upstashBoxSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-upstash-box'\n\nconst box = upstashBoxSandbox({ apiKey: process.env.UPSTASH_BOX_API_KEY })","lang":"ts"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Isolation:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" a managed "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/upstash/box","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Upstash Box"}]},{"type":"text","value":" cloud\nsandbox, a remote container you do not run yourself."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Auth / env:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" needs "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"UPSTASH_BOX_API_KEY"},{"type":"text","value":" (or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"apiKey"},{"type":"text","value":"); override the API\nbase with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"baseUrl"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"UPSTASH_BOX_BASE_URL"},{"type":"text","value":". Pick the image and size with\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runtime"},{"type":"text","value":" (default "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"node"},{"type":"text","value":") and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"size"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Paths:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the conventional "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/workspace"},{"type":"text","value":" virtual root maps to the box home,\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/workspace/home"},{"type":"text","value":", which is the handle's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"workspaceRoot"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Processes:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"spawn()"},{"type":"text","value":" opens a live "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"exec.session"},{"type":"text","value":" over a WebSocket, so a\nbackground process has a real in-box pid, a writable stdin, separate stdout and\nstderr, and server-side signals. A session owns its process: dropping the\nconnection kills the command and sessions cannot be reattached, so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"spawn()"},{"type":"text","value":" is\nscoped to the lifetime of the handle rather than the box. Blocking "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"exec()"},{"type":"text","value":"\nstays on the HTTP path and is shell-wrapped for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cwd"},{"type":"text","value":"/env, which the session\ntakes natively."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Snapshot / resume:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"snapshot()"},{"type":"text","value":" calls "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"box.snapshot()"},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"restoreSnapshot()"},{"type":"text","value":" reconstructs a new box from it via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Box.fromSnapshot()"},{"type":"text","value":", so\na snapshot survives deletion of the box that made it. Resume-by-id uses\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Box.get"},{"type":"text","value":" (id or name) and probes "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getStatus"},{"type":"text","value":", so a deleted record resumes as\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":" rather than a tombstone handle."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ports:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ports.connect(port)"},{"type":"text","value":" mints a preview URL via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getPublicURL"},{"type":"text","value":". Pass\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"publicUrlAuth"},{"type":"text","value":" to gate it, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ bearerToken: true }"},{"type":"text","value":" returns a token plus an\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Authorization: Bearer"},{"type":"text","value":" header and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ basicAuth: true }"},{"type":"text","value":" returns Basic\ncredentials; without it the preview URL is unauthenticated."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Network:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"policy.capabilities.network"},{"type":"text","value":" of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'deny'"},{"type":"text","value":" maps to Box's\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"deny-all"},{"type":"text","value":" egress mode. The contract's gate is coarse, so Box's domain and CIDR\nallowlists are not reachable through it. This is stricter than providers that\nmodel deny as an allowlist: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"deny-all"},{"type":"text","value":" blocks every outbound connection, so an\nagent that works under an allowlist-style deny will not reach package\nregistries or model provider hosts here. Leave the capability unset if the\nagent needs either."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fork:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fork()"},{"type":"text","value":" snapshots the box and creates a new one from that snapshot,\nthe same shape as Docker's commit plus create. It costs a full snapshot round\ntrip (about 25 seconds), unlike Docker's local commit."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bridge:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" like Daytona and Vercel, it is a remote VM, so bridged tools need\nthe tunnel in local dev (see "},{"type":"link","href":"./tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":")."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"blaxel","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Blaxel"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { blaxelSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-blaxel'\n\nconst blaxel = blaxelSandbox({\n  apiKey: process.env.BL_API_KEY,\n  workspace: process.env.BL_WORKSPACE,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Isolation:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" a managed "},{"type":"link","href":"https://blaxel.ai","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Blaxel"}]},{"type":"text","value":" cloud sandbox — a remote VM\nyou don't run yourself. Pick the image with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"image"},{"type":"text","value":" (default\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"blaxel/base-image:latest"},{"type":"text","value":") and the size with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"memory"},{"type":"text","value":" (default 2048 MB). Set\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"region"},{"type":"text","value":" (or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BL_REGION"},{"type":"text","value":") to choose a region and to silence the SDK's warning\nthat it will become required."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Auth / env:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"apiKey"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"workspace"},{"type":"text","value":" as constructor options, or set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BL_API_KEY"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BL_WORKSPACE"},{"type":"text","value":". If you omit the API key, the provider uses the SDK CLI login or client credentials. A requested workspace must match those credentials. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@blaxel/core"},{"type":"text","value":" authentication is process-global. The provider records the resolved workspace at construction and rejects a later provider that asks for a different workspace."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lifetime:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" created sandboxes carry a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1h"},{"type":"text","value":" TTL by default so an abandoned run\ncannot strand a paid sandbox. Override with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ttl"},{"type":"text","value":", or pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ttl: null"},{"type":"text","value":" to manage\nlifetime yourself."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resume:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" resume-by-id reconnects to the named sandbox, and its filesystem\nis durable across idle suspend/resume for the sandbox's lifetime. Blaxel's\nsnapshot/fork API is currently a source-scoped private preview, has no\nentitlement probe, and does not document snapshots surviving source deletion.\nThe framework requires "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"snapshots"},{"type":"text","value":" to reconstruct after the source is gone, so\nthis provider conservatively advertises both "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"snapshots"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fork"},{"type":"text","value":" as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":"\nand does not expose "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"restoreSnapshot"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ports:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ports.connect(port)"},{"type":"text","value":" creates a per-port preview URL. Previews are\ntoken-gated by default and the returned channel carries both the token and the\nready-to-send "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"X-Blaxel-Preview-Token"},{"type":"text","value":" header. Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"publicPreviews: true"},{"type":"text","value":" for\nunauthenticated URLs."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Files:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fs.watch()"},{"type":"text","value":" is native, so file-event and diff hooks work without polling. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fs.lstat()"},{"type":"text","value":" reports file, directory, and symlink metadata without following links; missing paths return "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":", while other errors propagate. Custom images must provide GNU "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stat"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Process output:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" stdout and stderr remain live-streamed through bounded\nremote capture pipelines. Concurrent stdout and stderr use labeled records on\none transport stream, including across keepalive boundaries. Each stream has an 8 MiB total limit; exceeding it\nfails and remotely reaps the process instead of accumulating unbounded logs in\nthe provider host. Cancellation uses the same process-group supervisor because\nthe pinned SDK does not prove named-process kill reaches child processes.\nCustom images must provide Bash plus "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cat"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mkfifo"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dd"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"base64"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tr"},{"type":"text","value":",\nand "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"wc"},{"type":"text","value":" (the default Blaxel base image does). The supervisor invokes Bash\nexplicitly so job-control process groups do not depend on the image's\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/bin/sh"},{"type":"text","value":" implementation."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resume semantics:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" a destroyed sandbox does not disappear immediately —\nBlaxel keeps the record in a teardown state before purging it. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resume()"},{"type":"text","value":"\ntreats deleting, deactivating, failed, and terminated records as gone, while a\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DEACTIVATED"},{"type":"text","value":" sandbox remains resumable consistently with the pinned SDK."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bridge:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" like Daytona/Vercel, a remote VM — bridged tools need the tunnel in\nlocal dev (see "},{"type":"link","href":"./tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":")."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"boxd","children":[{"type":"text","value":"boxd"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { boxdSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-boxd'\n\nconst boxd = boxdSandbox({\n  apiKey: process.env.BOXD_API_KEY,\n  org: 'acme',\n  vcpu: 2,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Isolation:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" a managed "},{"type":"link","href":"https://boxd.sh","children":[{"type":"text","value":"boxd"}]},{"type":"text","value":" KVM microVM, a remote VM\nyou do not run yourself. Every machine is created "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isolated"},{"type":"text","value":": no in-VM\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"boxd"},{"type":"text","value":" CLI, no metadata endpoint, no org integrations, and no peers on the\norg network. The image is Ubuntu 24.04 with Node 24, Python 3, git, Docker,\nand the Claude Code and Codex CLIs preinstalled."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Auth / env:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" needs "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BOXD_API_KEY"},{"type":"text","value":" (or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"apiKey"},{"type":"text","value":") and the org the key\nbelongs to ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"org"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BOXD_ORG"},{"type":"text","value":"). Override the endpoint with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"baseUrl"},{"type":"text","value":" /\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BOXD_BASE_URL"},{"type":"text","value":". Harness credentials are injected as workspace secrets and\ntravel as per-command env. The API key never enters the machine."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Size:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" pick "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"vcpu"},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"2"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"4"},{"type":"text","value":"). boxd resolves memory from it:\n4, 8, or 16 GiB. The default is the org's default size. Every machine has a\n100 GB disk."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Working directory:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the portable root "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/workspace"},{"type":"text","value":" maps to\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/home/boxd/workspace"},{"type":"text","value":". Override with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"workdir"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cancellation:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" if you abort create or snapshot restore during startup,\nthe provider tries to delete the new machine after the current SDK call\nfinishes."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Processes:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"spawn()"},{"type":"text","value":" opens a streaming exec with separate stdout and\nstderr and a writable stdin. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"kill()"},{"type":"text","value":" signals the process group inside the\nmachine and verifies that it is gone, so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"killableProcesses"},{"type":"text","value":" is measured,\nnot assumed."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Snapshot / resume:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"snapshot()"},{"type":"text","value":" captures memory and disk into a boxd\nsnapshot named "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<machine>-<label>"},{"type":"text","value":" and waits until it is restorable (about\n25 s for an 8 GiB machine). "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"restoreSnapshot()"},{"type":"text","value":" boots a new machine from it\nin about 1 s, with the captured processes still running. Resume-by-id\nreconnects to the same machine across stop (2 to 3 s to start), suspend\n(about 140 ms) and hibernate."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fork:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fork()"},{"type":"text","value":" is a live boxd fork: disk, memory and running processes,\nready in under a second."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lifetime:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" a machine is persistent until "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"destroy()"},{"type":"text","value":". It suspends after\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"autoSuspendTimeout"},{"type":"text","value":" idle seconds and hibernates after 4 hours idle by\ndefault, at no compute cost, and wakes on the next command. Idle means no\ninbound connection. Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"autoDestroyTimeout"},{"type":"text","value":" as a safety net for abandoned\nsandboxes. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stop"},{"type":"text","value":" is a power-off: a file written seconds before it can still\nsit in the page cache and be lost, so run "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sync"},{"type":"text","value":" before you stop a machine\nyourself. Suspend, hibernate, snapshot and fork keep memory, so they do not\nlose it."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ports:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" every machine has one public HTTPS URL, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"https://<name>.boxd.sh"},{"type":"text","value":".\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ports.connect(port)"},{"type":"text","value":" pins that URL to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"port"},{"type":"text","value":" and returns it. The URL is\npublic: anyone who has it can reach the port."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Golden image:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fromSnapshot"},{"type":"text","value":" to boot every new sandbox from a\nsnapshot you baked after "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setup"},{"type":"text","value":", instead of the default image. The size is\nthen fixed by the snapshot."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bridge:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" like the other cloud providers, it is a remote VM, so bridged\ntools need the tunnel in local dev (see "},{"type":"link","href":"./tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":")."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"e2b","children":[{"type":"text","value":"E2B"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { e2bSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-e2b'\n\nconst e2b = e2bSandbox({ apiKey: process.env.E2B_API_KEY })","lang":"ts"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Isolation:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" a managed "},{"type":"link","href":"https://e2b.dev","children":[{"type":"text","value":"E2B"}]},{"type":"text","value":" sandbox, a Firecracker microVM\nyou do not run yourself. Pick the image with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"template"},{"type":"text","value":" (default: the E2B\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"base"},{"type":"text","value":" template)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Auth / env:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" needs "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"E2B_API_KEY"},{"type":"text","value":" (or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"apiKey"},{"type":"text","value":"). Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"domain"},{"type":"text","value":" (or\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"E2B_DOMAIN"},{"type":"text","value":") for a self-hosted or BYOC deployment. Harness credentials are\ninjected as "},{"type":"link","href":"./provisioning","children":[{"type":"text","value":"workspace secrets"}]},{"type":"text","value":". At create and snapshot\nrestore they are sent as sandbox "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"envs"},{"type":"text","value":". Per-command "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"env"},{"type":"text","value":" goes through the\nSDK's native "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"envs"},{"type":"text","value":" argument. No value is written into a command string."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lifetime:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" a sandbox lives for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"timeoutMs"},{"type":"text","value":" (default 30 minutes) and is\nkilled when that time elapses, so an abandoned run cannot keep billing.\nResume extends the lifetime by the same amount. Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onTimeout: 'pause'"},{"type":"text","value":" to\nkeep a timed-out sandbox resumable instead. E2B caps the lifetime at 1 hour\non the Hobby plan and 24 hours on Pro."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Snapshot / resume:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"snapshot()"},{"type":"text","value":" calls "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createSnapshot()"},{"type":"text","value":". The sandbox is\npaused briefly, then resumed. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"restoreSnapshot()"},{"type":"text","value":" creates a new sandbox from\nthat snapshot, so a snapshot survives deletion of its source. Resume-by-id\nuses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Sandbox.connect"},{"type":"text","value":", which also wakes a paused sandbox. A killed or\nexpired sandbox resumes as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fork:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fork()"},{"type":"text","value":" is native. The parent is checkpointed in place and the copy\nboots from that checkpoint."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Processes:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" every command runs as the leader of its own process group\n("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setsid"},{"type":"text","value":"), so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"kill()"},{"type":"text","value":" reaches backgrounded children. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"kill()"},{"type":"text","value":" always sends\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SIGKILL"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"spawn()"},{"type":"text","value":" has a real sandbox pid, a writable stdin, and separate\nstdout and stderr. A custom template must include "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setsid"},{"type":"text","value":" (util-linux); the\ndefault template has it."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ports:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ports.connect(port)"},{"type":"text","value":" returns\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"https://<port>-<sandbox-id>.<domain>"},{"type":"text","value":". With "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"allowPublicTraffic: false"},{"type":"text","value":" the\nURL is gated by the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"e2b-traffic-access-token"},{"type":"text","value":" header, and the channel carries\nthat header in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"headers"},{"type":"text","value":". Browsers cannot send it, so leave public traffic on\nfor preview links a person clicks."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Network:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"policy.capabilities.network: 'deny'"},{"type":"text","value":" maps to\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"allowInternetAccess: false"},{"type":"text","value":", which blocks all outbound traffic. E2B's\nper-host allow and deny lists are not reachable through the contract's coarse\ngate."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Paths:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the portable root "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/workspace"},{"type":"text","value":" maps to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/home/user/workspace"},{"type":"text","value":" by\ndefault. Override with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"workdir"},{"type":"text","value":". The sandbox user is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"user"},{"type":"text","value":" (not root) with\npasswordless "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sudo"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/workspace"},{"type":"text","value":" itself is not writable."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bridge:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" like Daytona and Vercel, it is a remote VM, so bridged tools need\nthe tunnel in local dev (see "},{"type":"link","href":"./tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":")."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"railway","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Railway"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { railwaySandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-railway'\n\nconst railway = railwaySandbox({\n  environmentId: process.env.RAILWAY_ENVIRONMENT_ID,\n  region: 'us-west2',\n  idleTimeoutMinutes: 30,\n  resources: { cpu: 2, memoryGB: 4 },\n  // Optional: publish preview domains. Requires PRIVATE networking.\n  networkIsolation: 'PRIVATE',\n  ports: [3000],\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Isolation:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" a managed "},{"type":"link","href":"https://railway.com","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Railway"}]},{"type":"text","value":" sandbox, a VM you do\nnot run yourself, created in a Railway environment. Boot from the base image,\na named "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"checkpoint"},{"type":"text","value":", or a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Sandbox.template()"},{"type":"text","value":" recipe ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"template"},{"type":"text","value":")."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Auth / env:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the SDK reads "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RAILWAY_TOKEN"},{"type":"text","value":" (a project token) or\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RAILWAY_API_TOKEN"},{"type":"text","value":" (an account or workspace token). An explicit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"token"},{"type":"text","value":"\ndefaults to bearer auth; pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"authType: 'project-token'"},{"type":"text","value":" for a project\ntoken. Scope is the environment ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"environmentId"},{"type":"text","value":" or\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RAILWAY_ENVIRONMENT_ID"},{"type":"text","value":"), frozen per provider. Harness credentials are\ninjected as "},{"type":"link","href":"./provisioning","children":[{"type":"text","value":"workspace secrets"}]},{"type":"text","value":": at create and restore they\nare sent as the sandbox's runtime "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"env"},{"type":"text","value":", and per-command "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"env"},{"type":"text","value":" travels in the\nexec init frame. No value is written into a command string."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Private networking:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"networkIsolation: 'PRIVATE'"},{"type":"text","value":" the sandbox joins\nthe environment's private network, so the agent can reach your other Railway\nservices (databases, internal APIs) by their private hostnames. The default,\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ISOLATED"},{"type":"text","value":", has no private-network access."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lifetime:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Railway destroys a sandbox after "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"idleTimeoutMinutes"},{"type":"text","value":" of\ninactivity (plan default when omitted; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0"},{"type":"text","value":" disables idle destruction where\nthe plan allows it). There is no stop/start that keeps the disk, so\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durableFilesystem"},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":": use a checkpoint or\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./portable-snapshots","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Portable Snapshots"}]},{"type":"text","value":" to keep work across idle expiry.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SandboxCreateInput"},{"type":"text","value":" carries no lifecycle hint, so set the timeout here.\nResume resets the idle countdown."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Size:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resources: { cpu, memoryGB }"},{"type":"text","value":" sets vCPU (fractions allowed) and\nmemory in decimal GB for every create, restore, and fork. Omitted fields use\nthe workspace default; values above its maximum fail at create."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Snapshot / resume:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"snapshot(label)"},{"type":"text","value":" captures a native Railway checkpoint\nnamed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tsai-<sandbox-id>-<uuid>"},{"type":"text","value":". The label stays on the returned ref only,\nbecause checkpoint names are environment-scoped and a capture replaces an\nexisting name. Checkpoints belong to the environment, not the sandbox, so\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"restoreSnapshot()"},{"type":"text","value":" boots a new sandbox from one after its source is\ndestroyed, and reapplies env, network mode, domains, and idle timeout from\nthe provider config. Restores and forks run in the source region, so\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"region"},{"type":"text","value":" applies to fresh sandboxes only. Checkpoints count\nagainst a per-plan quota and the contract has no delete hook: prune\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tsai-*"},{"type":"text","value":" checkpoints you no longer reference with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Sandbox.checkpoints()"},{"type":"text","value":"\nand "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Sandbox.deleteCheckpoint()"},{"type":"text","value":" from the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"railway"},{"type":"text","value":" SDK. Resume-by-id uses\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Sandbox.connect"},{"type":"text","value":". A missing, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DESTROYED"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DESTROYING"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"FAILED"},{"type":"text","value":" sandbox\nresumes as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":", a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"CREATING"},{"type":"text","value":" one is awaited, and auth or transport errors\nare rethrown."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fork:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fork()"},{"type":"text","value":" is a native live disk fork of the running sandbox into a\nnew one in the same environment and region. Processes are not copied. The\nfork gets the parent's network mode, domains, idle timeout, and a copy of\nits env overlay."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Processes:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" stdout and stderr stay separate. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"spawn()"},{"type":"text","value":" runs in a durable\nexec session with a flow-controlled stdin writer, so it keeps running if the\nhost disconnects; this provider does not expose reattach, because TanStack's\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./journal","children":[{"type":"text","value":"run journal"}]},{"type":"text","value":" owns run identity. Blocking "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"exec"},{"type":"text","value":" runs without a\ndurable session and is killed if the connection drops. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"spawn()"},{"type":"text","value":" reports\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pid: -1"},{"type":"text","value":" because the exec bridge exposes no remote pid. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"kill()"},{"type":"text","value":" and an\naborted "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"exec"},{"type":"text","value":" send "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TERM"},{"type":"text","value":" to the process group and escalate to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"KILL"},{"type":"text","value":" after\n2 s. An aborted "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"spawn"},{"type":"text","value":" sends "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"KILL"},{"type":"text","value":" at once. All of them settle on the\nconfirmed remote exit. An aborted "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"exec"},{"type":"text","value":" resolves with the signalled exit\ncode."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cancellation:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" if create fails or times out after Railway minted the\nsandbox, or the caller aborts while it boots, the provider tries to destroy\nit. Both are best effort."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ports:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Railway publishes HTTP domains at create time only. Declare them\nwith "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ports"},{"type":"text","value":" (at most 10), which requires "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"networkIsolation: 'PRIVATE'"},{"type":"text","value":";\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ports.connect(port)"},{"type":"text","value":" returns "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"https://<domain>"},{"type":"text","value":" for a declared port and\nrejects any other. Without "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ports"},{"type":"text","value":", the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ports"},{"type":"text","value":" capability is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":". The\ndomain is public: it carries no preview token."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Network:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"networkPolicy"},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":". Both modes keep public internet\negress, so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"policy.capabilities.network: 'deny'"},{"type":"text","value":" is rejected at create\ninstead of being dropped."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Paths:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the portable root "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/workspace"},{"type":"text","value":" is a real directory created at\nboot. Override with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"workdir"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fs.remove"},{"type":"text","value":" is recursive ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rm -rf"},{"type":"text","value":") and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fs.lstat"},{"type":"text","value":" is a GNU "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stat"},{"type":"text","value":" probe that does not follow symlinks."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bridge:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" like the other cloud providers, it is a remote VM, so bridged\ntools need the tunnel in local dev (see "},{"type":"link","href":"./tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":")."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"capabilities","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Capabilities"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Providers declare what they support via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"capabilities()"},{"type":"text","value":". The flags are:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Capability"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Meaning"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fs"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Read/write the sandbox filesystem."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"exec"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Run commands."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"env"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Inject environment variables."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ports"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Expose/forward ports (preview URLs)."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"backgroundProcesses"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep long-running processes alive between calls."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"writableStdin"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A spawned process exposes a writable host→process stdin. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" for local-process, Docker container, Daytona, Upstash Box, E2B, boxd, and Railway. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":" for Docker Sandboxes ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sbx"},{"type":"text","value":"), Vercel, Sprites, Blaxel, and Cloudflare. When "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":", stdin-fed harnesses write the prompt to a file and redirect it in the shell."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"killableProcesses"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A spawned process can be forcibly stopped via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SpawnHandle.kill()"},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"and"}]},{"type":"text","value":" aborted mid-flight via the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"signal"},{"type":"text","value":" passed to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"spawn"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"snapshots"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Capture and restore point-in-time snapshots."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"networkPolicy"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Enforce network allow/deny rules."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durableFilesystem"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disk that survives across resumes."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fork"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Branch a sandbox from an existing one."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Code that uses an "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"optional"}]},{"type":"text","value":" capability checks the flag first and degrades\ngracefully. For example, bootstrap only snapshots when "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"snapshots"},{"type":"text","value":" is supported,\nso "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"localProcessSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":" simply skips the step. Calling an unsupported optional\nmethod directly (instead of checking the flag) throws an\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"UnsupportedCapabilityError"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { localProcessSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-local-process'\n\nconst provider = localProcessSandbox()\nconst caps = provider.capabilities()\n\nif (caps.snapshots) {\n  // safe to take a snapshot\n} else {\n  // degrade gracefully, local-process has no snapshots\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the flags to write provider-agnostic code: branch on the capability rather\nthan the concrete provider, and your sandbox definition keeps working when you\nswap one provider for another."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"killableprocesses-across-the-bundled-providers","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"killableProcesses"},{"type":"text","value":" across the bundled providers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This flag is "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"measured, not asserted"}]},{"type":"text","value":". A wrong "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" hands the journal reader\nan unstoppable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tail -f"},{"type":"text","value":" and leaks a process per run, so a provider only declares\nit once killing has been observed to work against a real sandbox. Two of these\ndeclarations were once "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" on reasoning alone and both turned out to be false\nwhen probed (Docker's stream destroy left the container-side process in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ps"},{"type":"text","value":";\nlocal-process's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sh -c"},{"type":"text","value":" did not "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"exec"},{"type":"text","value":", so killing the shell left the command\nalive). Anything that cannot be measured yet stays "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":", because "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"poll"},{"type":"text","value":" is\nmerely slower while a wrong "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"follow"},{"type":"text","value":" is a leak."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Provider"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"killableProcesses"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Why"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Local process"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Measured."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Kills the process GROUP, not the wrapper: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"detached"},{"type":"text","value":" spawn plus "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"process.kill(-pid, signal)"},{"type":"text","value":" on POSIX (killing only the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sh"},{"type":"text","value":" left the command running, dash does not reliably "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"exec"},{"type":"text","value":"); on Windows "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"taskkill /T"},{"type":"text","value":" plus a verified sweep, see "},{"type":"link","href":"#windows-process-teardown-logger","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Windows teardown"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Docker"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Measured."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Signals the process INSIDE the container by the pid the wrapper recorded for itself, process group first and escalating to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"KILL"},{"type":"text","value":". Destroying the hijacked exec stream is "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"not"}]},{"type":"text","value":" sufficient: it only detaches the client."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Docker Sandboxes ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sbx"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Unmeasured. Live tests named-skip until "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sbx login"},{"type":"text","value":". The handle records a pid in the VM, but "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"killableProcesses"},{"type":"text","value":" stays "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":" until that kill is observed."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Daytona"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"kill()"},{"type":"text","value":" only aborts the client-side poll loop and does not await any termination; the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"deleteSession"},{"type":"text","value":" that might terminate the command runs later from the pump's teardown, is failure-swallowed, and is documented as cleanup for a "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"completed"}]},{"type":"text","value":" session. Unmeasured, needs "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DAYTONA_API_KEY"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The abort signal reaches only the HTTP request that STARTS a detached command, so the old "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"kill()"},{"type":"text","value":" was a no-op. It now issues the SDK's server-side "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Command.kill"},{"type":"text","value":", but whether that reaches a forked child (the follow command is a multi-statement shell, so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tail -f"},{"type":"text","value":" is always a child) is unmeasured, needs Vercel credentials."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sprites"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" (unverified)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not a client-side detach: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"kill()"},{"type":"text","value":" issues a real server-side "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"POST /exec/<sessionId>/kill"},{"type":"text","value":" before closing the socket. What that endpoint signals (process group or pid) is undocumented and unmeasured; needs "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SPRITES_API_KEY"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Upstash Box"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Measured."}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"kill()"},{"type":"text","value":" sends an allowlisted signal ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TERM"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"KILL"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"INT"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HUP"},{"type":"text","value":") that the box agent delivers to the process TREE server-side, so a forked child is signalled too. Verified against production: a spawned "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sleep 5 && touch <marker>"},{"type":"text","value":" was killed and the marker never appeared. Needs "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"UPSTASH_BOX_API_KEY"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Blaxel"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The provider reaps supervisor and child process groups, then waits for the remote reaper to finish. Credential-gated journal conformance verifies that cancellation leaves no active follower process."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"E2B"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Measured."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The SDK's own kill is a SIGKILL to the shell pid, and a backgrounded "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"( … ) & wait"},{"type":"text","value":" child survived it. Every command therefore runs as a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setsid"},{"type":"text","value":" group leader and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"kill()"},{"type":"text","value":" runs "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"kill -KILL -- -<pid>"},{"type":"text","value":" inside the sandbox. The shared journal conformance kill case passes against a real sandbox. Needs "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"E2B_API_KEY"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cloudflare"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"kill()"},{"type":"text","value":" is a no-op, and the caller's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AbortSignal"},{"type":"text","value":" reaches neither "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"exec"},{"type":"text","value":" nor "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"spawn"},{"type":"text","value":", because Workers RPC cannot serialize one."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"boxd"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Measured."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The spawn wrapper runs under "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setsid"},{"type":"text","value":", so the pid it records leads its own process group. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"kill()"},{"type":"text","value":" runs a shell inside the machine that signals that group, escalates to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"KILL"},{"type":"text","value":", and checks with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"kill -0"},{"type":"text","value":". Closing the stream alone is not a kill: the process survived it. Verified against production: a spawned "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sleep 5 && touch <marker>"},{"type":"text","value":" was killed and the marker never appeared. Needs "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BOXD_API_KEY"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Railway"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Measured."}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"kill()"},{"type":"text","value":" and an aborted "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"exec"},{"type":"text","value":" send "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TERM"},{"type":"text","value":" to the command's process group and escalate to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"KILL"},{"type":"text","value":" after 2 s. An aborted "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"spawn"},{"type":"text","value":" sends "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"KILL"},{"type":"text","value":" at once. Each settles only when the remote exit is confirmed. Verified against production: the shared journal conformance kill case passes, and spawned "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sleep 5 && touch <marker>"},{"type":"text","value":" commands (including a backgrounded child) were killed before the marker appeared, for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"kill()"},{"type":"text","value":", an aborted "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"exec"},{"type":"text","value":", and an aborted "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"spawn"},{"type":"text","value":". Needs "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RAILWAY_API_TOKEN"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RAILWAY_TOKEN"},{"type":"text","value":" + "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RAILWAY_ENVIRONMENT_ID"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each of the remote providers registers the shared journal conformance suite, so\nthe claim is falsifiable rather than asserted: with credentials present the suite\nruns against a real sandbox, and without them it reports a "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"named skip"}]},{"type":"text","value":" carrying\nthe reason instead of a silent pass. Cloudflare's gate is the runtime rather than\ncredentials, its provider can only create a sandbox through a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Sandbox"},{"type":"text","value":" Durable\nObject binding, which no Node test process has, so its registration is a named\nskip saying exactly that, until a Workers-runtime harness can measure it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This flag is required on every provider, including a bring-your-own one. A\nprovider that omitted it would be treated as killable, which is the dangerous\ndefault: a follower process started there could never be reclaimed, and it would\nkeep running inside the sandbox for as long as the sandbox lives."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It is the flag the "},{"type":"link","href":"./journal","children":[{"type":"text","value":"run journal"}]},{"type":"text","value":" reads to decide how to tail a run's\noutput: a killable provider gets a streaming "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tail -f"},{"type":"text","value":", and a provider like\nCloudflare gets a loop of bounded reads, each of which terminates on its own."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The flag also bounds what "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"cancel"}]},{"type":"text","value":" can mean. On a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":" provider there is no\nsignal path to the agent process, so the only cancel that actually stops the\nagent means destroying the sandbox, which is what the cancel path does. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./takeover#what-cancel-means-on-a-provider-that-cannot-kill","children":[{"type":"text","value":"what cancel means on a provider that cannot kill"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"choosing-a-provider","text":"Choosing a provider","level":2},{"id":"local-process","text":"Local process","level":2},{"id":"host-login-vs-api-key-scrubenv","text":"Host login vs API key (scrubEnv)","level":3},{"id":"windows-process-teardown-logger","text":"Windows process teardown (logger)","level":3},{"id":"docker","text":"Docker","level":2},{"id":"docker-sandboxes-sbx","text":"Docker Sandboxes (sbx)","level":2},{"id":"daytona","text":"Daytona","level":2},{"id":"vercel","text":"Vercel","level":2},{"id":"sprites","text":"Sprites","level":2},{"id":"upstash-box","text":"Upstash Box","level":2},{"id":"blaxel","text":"Blaxel","level":2},{"id":"boxd","text":"boxd","level":2},{"id":"e2b","text":"E2B","level":2},{"id":"railway","text":"Railway","level":2},{"id":"capabilities","text":"Capabilities","level":2},{"id":"killableprocesses-across-the-bundled-providers","text":"killableProcesses across the bundled providers","level":3}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nA provider owns the isolation primitive: where the harness actually runs. Every\nprovider implements the same `SandboxProvider` / `SandboxHandle` contract, so the\n[workspace](./workspace) you hand the agent and the [policy](./policy) that guards\nit are provider-agnostic. Pick a provider for the isolation, auth, and\nsnapshot/resume behaviour you need; the rest of your sandbox definition stays the\nsame.\n\nProvider-native snapshots and resume keep or recreate provider state. They can\nreduce bootstrap time. [Portable Snapshots](./portable-snapshots) store\ncompleted workspace data in your application persistence for reconstruction.\n\n> The provider is _where_ the agent runs. For _which_ agent runs (Grok Build,\n> Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, or any ACP agent via `acpCompatible`) see\n> [Harnesses](./harnesses).\n\n## Choosing a provider\n\n| Provider | Package | Isolation | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Local process | `@tanstack/ai-sandbox-local-process` | none (host) | The fast, no-Docker dev loop. Trusted/dev use only. |\n| Docker | `@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker` | container | Real isolation; commit-based snapshots, fork, resume-by-id. |\n| Docker Sandboxes (`sbx`) | `@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker` | microVM | Native Docker Sandboxes. Same package as `dockerSandbox()`, different export: `sbxSandbox()`. Needs the `sbx` CLI, a login, and a hypervisor. |\n| Daytona | `@tanstack/ai-sandbox-daytona` | cloud sandbox | Managed [Daytona](https://www.daytona.io/) sandboxes; snapshots after setup, port preview links, resume-by-id. Needs `DAYTONA_API_KEY`. |\n| Vercel | `@tanstack/ai-sandbox-vercel` | microVM | Managed [Vercel Sandbox](https://vercel.com/docs/sandbox) microVMs; exposed-port domains, resume-by-id (persistent). Needs `VERCEL_TOKEN` + team/project. |\n| Sprites | `@tanstack/ai-sandbox-sprites` | stateful sandbox | Managed [Sprites](https://sprites.dev) (Fly.io) sandboxes; durable filesystem, in-place checkpoints, single proxied public-URL port, resume-by-id. Needs `SPRITES_API_KEY`. |\n| Upstash Box | `@tanstack/ai-sandbox-upstash-box` | cloud sandbox | Managed [Upstash Box](https://github.com/upstash/box) sandboxes; interactive processes over a WebSocket session (real pid, stdin, signals), native snapshots, preview URLs, resume-by-id. Needs `UPSTASH_BOX_API_KEY`. |\n| Blaxel | `@tanstack/ai-sandbox-blaxel` | cloud sandbox | Managed [Blaxel](https://blaxel.ai) sandboxes; durable filesystem, per-port preview URLs, native file watch, resume-by-id. Snapshot/fork remain disabled while the source-scoped private-preview semantics are unproven. Accepts `BL_API_KEY` + `BL_WORKSPACE` or SDK-resolved CLI or client credentials. |\n| E2B | `@tanstack/ai-sandbox-e2b` | microVM | Managed [E2B](https://e2b.dev) Firecracker sandboxes. Native snapshots and fork, preview URLs, writable stdin, process-group kill, resume-by-id (also wakes a paused sandbox). Needs `E2B_API_KEY`. |\n| boxd | `@tanstack/ai-sandbox-boxd` | microVM | Managed [boxd](https://boxd.sh) KVM microVMs; live fork (memory and processes), snapshots that restore into a new machine, persistent disk, resume-by-id across stop, suspend and hibernate, one public HTTPS URL per machine. Needs `BOXD_API_KEY` + `BOXD_ORG`. |\n| Railway | `@tanstack/ai-sandbox-railway` | microVM | Managed [Railway](https://railway.com) sandboxes. Private networking to your other Railway services, live disk fork, environment-scoped checkpoints that survive deletion of their source, durable exec sessions, create-time HTTP domains, resume-by-id. Needs `RAILWAY_API_TOKEN` or `RAILWAY_TOKEN` + an environment. |\n\nMost providers are their own package. `dockerSandbox()` and `sbxSandbox()` both\ncome from `@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker`. The constructor is the only thing that\ndiffers between them:\n\n```ts\nimport { localProcessSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-local-process'\nimport { dockerSandbox, sbxSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker'\nimport { daytonaSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-daytona'\nimport { vercelSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-vercel'\nimport { upstashBoxSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-upstash-box'\nimport { blaxelSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-blaxel'\nimport { e2bSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-e2b'\nimport { boxdSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-boxd'\nimport { railwaySandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-railway'\n\nconst dev = localProcessSandbox() // runs on your host\nconst isolated = dockerSandbox({ image: 'node:22' }) // container\nconst microvm = sbxSandbox() // Docker Sandboxes microVM\nconst daytona = daytonaSandbox({ apiKey: process.env.DAYTONA_API_KEY }) // managed cloud sandbox\nconst vercel = vercelSandbox({ runtime: 'node24' }) // managed Vercel microVM\nconst box = upstashBoxSandbox({ apiKey: process.env.UPSTASH_BOX_API_KEY }) // managed Upstash Box\nconst blaxel = blaxelSandbox() // managed Blaxel sandbox; uses API-key or CLI credentials\nconst e2b = e2bSandbox() // managed E2B microVM; reads E2B_API_KEY\nconst boxd = boxdSandbox({ org: 'acme' }) // managed boxd microVM; reads BOXD_API_KEY\nconst railway = railwaySandbox() // managed Railway sandbox; reads RAILWAY_TOKEN + RAILWAY_ENVIRONMENT_ID\n```\n\n> Cloud providers (including Daytona, Vercel, Sprites, Upstash Box, Blaxel, E2B, boxd, and Railway)\n> run remotely. When you drive them from your laptop, [tools](./tools) bridged\n> from `chat()` can't dial your machine's\n> `localhost`, you need the bridge tunnel. See the [tools guide](./tools) for the\n> ngrok subpath, and the [Cloudflare guide](./cloudflare) for the edge-native\n> co-located model.\n\n## Local process\n\n```ts\nimport { localProcessSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-local-process'\n\nconst dev = localProcessSandbox()\n```\n\n- **Isolation:** none. The harness runs directly on your host, inheriting your\n  host environment. Use it for trusted or dev work only. There is no boundary\n  between the agent and your machine.\n- **Auth / env:** inherits the host environment. Set `authMode` on the harness\n  (`'host'` or `'api-key'`). The provider does not pick this. See\n  [Harness Auth](./auth).\n- **Snapshot / resume:** no snapshots and no durable resume-by-id; each run\n  re-creates and re-bootstraps under the same identity. The snapshot step is\n  skipped silently (see [Capabilities](#capabilities)).\n\n### Host login vs API key (`scrubEnv`)\n\nThe provider is where the agent runs, not how it signs in. The default\n`authMode` is `'api-key'`. Set `'host'` when the machine already has a CLI\nlogin. A local-process run can be your laptop or a GitHub runner. See\n[Harness Auth](./auth).\n\n`localProcessSandbox` inherits the host environment, including any API keys\nexported there. If you set `authMode: 'host'`, pass `scrubEnv` so those keys\ndo not override the CLI login:\n\n```ts\nimport { localProcessSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-local-process'\n\nconst hostLogin = localProcessSandbox({\n  scrubEnv: ['XAI_API_KEY', 'GROK_API_KEY'],\n})\n```\n\nIf the same local-process sandbox runs on a CI machine, set\n`authMode: 'api-key'`. Then inject the key as a workspace secret. Isolated and\ncloud providers have no host CLI login. Use `authMode: 'api-key'` and\nworkspace secrets there.\n\n### Windows process teardown (`logger`)\n\nKilling a spawned process means killing the whole tree, and on Windows that takes\nmore than `taskkill /T`. Commands run through a git-bash `sh`, and MSYS's\nfork emulation runs the final command of a statement list, such as the\n`tail -f` behind a [journal](./journal) follow read, under an intermediate shell\nthat immediately exits. Windows never reparents, so the surviving process points\nat a dead parent and `taskkill /T`, which walks only live parent links, cannot\nreach it **while still exiting `0`**. Left alone, every follow read leaks a\nprocess for the life of the machine.\n\n`localProcessSandbox` therefore consults MSYS's own process table, which does\nkeep the logical parentage before killing, then kills any descendant `/T` missed.\nTeardown is total by construction: it never throws, because a throwing kill would\nstrand a run mid-flight. That means a kill it genuinely cannot complete (a\nprotected process, access denied) is otherwise invisible, so pass a `logger` to\nsee it:\n\n```ts\nimport { localProcessSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-local-process'\n\nconst dev = localProcessSandbox({\n  logger: {\n    warn: (message, meta) => console.warn(message, meta),\n  },\n})\n```\n\nAny object with a `warn(message, meta?)` method works, so the `InternalLogger`\nyour adapter already receives can be handed straight in. A process that had\nalready exited on its own is **not** a failure and is never reported.\n\nNothing here changes on POSIX, where `sh` really is the command's parent and\nsignalling the wrapper is enough.\n\n## Docker\n\n```ts\nimport { dockerSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker'\n\nconst isolated = dockerSandbox({ image: 'node:22' })\n```\n\n- **Isolation:** a real container boundary between the agent and your host.\n- **Auth / env:** no host login; provide credentials as workspace secrets, which\n  are injected into the container env at create/resume. The agent reaches host\n  tools over `host.docker.internal` (see [tools](./tools)).\n- **Snapshot / resume:** full commit-based snapshots, `fork`, and resume-by-id.\n  Bootstrap snapshots after `setup` completes, so subsequent runs resume from the\n  snapshot instead of re-running setup.\n\n## Docker Sandboxes (sbx)\n\n```ts\nimport { sbxSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker'\nimport { defineSandbox, defineWorkspace, githubRepo } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n\nconst isolated = sbxSandbox({\n  allowNetwork: ['*.npmjs.org', 'registry.npmjs.org'],\n})\n\nconst sandbox = defineSandbox({\n  id: 'repo-agent',\n  provider: isolated,\n  workspace: defineWorkspace({\n    source: githubRepo({ repo: 'owner/repo' }),\n    setup: ['pnpm install'],\n  }),\n})\n```\n\n- **Isolation:** a hypervisor microVM. The sandbox has its own kernel and its own Docker daemon. This is not `dockerSandbox()`, which starts a container.\n- **Needs:** `sbx` on `PATH`, `sbx login` (or a PAT piped to `sbx login --password-stdin` in CI), and a hypervisor (Hyper-V, Virtualization.framework, or KVM). A Docker socket is not enough.\n- **Workspace:** `sbx create --clone` copies a host Git repo into the VM. Pass `workspaceDir` that contains `.git`, or set `workspace.source` to a git URL. If there is no Git repo, create throws. There is no bind-mount fallback.\n- **Auth / env:** inject API keys as workspace secrets. v1 does not call `sbx secret`.\n- **Snapshot / resume:** no snapshots and no fork. Resume reconnects by name (`sbx ls`). A stopped sandbox starts again on the next `sbx exec`.\n- **Network:** this is the first provider with `networkPolicy: true`. See [Policy](./policy).\n\n## Daytona\n\n```ts\nimport { daytonaSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-daytona'\n\nconst daytona = daytonaSandbox({\n  apiKey: process.env.DAYTONA_API_KEY,\n  snapshot: 'daytona-medium',\n  autoStopInterval: 0,\n})\n```\n\n- **Isolation:** a managed cloud sandbox on a remote VM you do not run yourself.\n- **Auth / env:** needs `DAYTONA_API_KEY`. Put harness credentials in\n  [workspace secrets](./provisioning). At create and snapshot restore,\n  Daytona stores each value as an organization Secret and mounts a\n  placeholder in the sandbox env. The create record, the dashboard env\n  view, and session command strings do not contain the real value. Daytona\n  substitutes the value on outbound HTTPS requests. Per-command `opts.env`\n  uses `executeCommand`'s env argument or a sourced env file. It never\n  writes `export KEY=` prefixes into the command string.\n- **Snapshot / resume:** point-in-time snapshots after setup (default when\n  `lifecycle.snapshot` is `'after-setup'`). Pass `snapshot` on\n  `daytonaSandbox()` to pick the Daytona image (for example\n  `'daytona-medium'`). Resume starts a `stopped` or `archived` sandbox, then\n  returns the handle.\n- **Idle stop:** Daytona stops an idle sandbox after 15 minutes by default.\n  Set `autoStopInterval` in minutes to change that. Pass `0` to turn auto-stop\n  off. Set `ephemeral: true` to delete the sandbox when it stops.\n- **Network:** `policy.capabilities.network: 'deny'` blocks all outbound\n  network on create.\n- **Working directory:** the portable root `/workspace` maps to\n  `/home/daytona/workspace` by default. Override with `workdir` on\n  `daytonaSandbox()` if you need another path.\n- **Stdin:** spawned processes accept host stdin (`writableStdin: true`).\n- **Privileges:** the Daytona user is not root. Package installs in `setup`\n  must use `sudo -n` (for example `sudo -n apt-get install …`). Do not put\n  `sudo *` in a [policy](./policy) deny list for this provider.\n- **Bridge:** the sandbox is remote, so a [bridged tool](./tools) call cannot\n  reach your laptop's `localhost`. In local dev, tunnel the bridge (see\n  [tools](./tools)). A deployed orchestrator is reachable without a tunnel.\n\nDefault headless path on Daytona:\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { grokBuildText } from '@tanstack/ai-grok-build'\nimport {\n  defineSandbox,\n  defineSandboxPolicy,\n  defineWorkspace,\n  gitSkill,\n  githubRepo,\n  withSandbox,\n} from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { daytonaSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-daytona'\n\nconst sandbox = defineSandbox({\n  id: 'daytona-agent',\n  provider: daytonaSandbox({\n    apiKey: process.env.DAYTONA_API_KEY,\n    snapshot: 'daytona-medium',\n  }),\n  workspace: defineWorkspace({\n    source: githubRepo({ repo: 'owner/app' }),\n    skills: [gitSkill({ repo: 'owner/skills-pack' })],\n  }),\n  policy: defineSandboxPolicy({\n    default: 'allow',\n  }),\n})\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: grokBuildText('grok-build'),\n  messages: [{ role: 'user', content: 'List the project files.' }],\n  middleware: [withSandbox(sandbox)],\n})\n```\n\nHeadless Grok Build and Codex stay on auto-approve with `default: 'allow'`.\nIsolation is the Daytona VM. Use Claude Code when you need command-level deny.\n\n## Vercel\n\n```ts\nimport { vercelSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-vercel'\n\nconst vercel = vercelSandbox({ runtime: 'node24' })\n```\n\n- **Isolation:** a managed microVM (Vercel Sandbox).\n- **Auth / env:** needs `VERCEL_TOKEN` plus a team/project. Harness credentials\n  are injected as workspace secrets.\n- **Snapshot / resume:** persistent resume-by-id with a durable filesystem, plus\n  exposed-port domains for previews.\n- **Bridge:** like Daytona, it is a remote VM, so bridged tools need the tunnel in local\n  dev (see [tools](./tools)).\n\n## Sprites\n\n```ts\nimport { spritesSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-sprites'\n\nconst sprites = spritesSandbox({ apiKey: process.env.SPRITES_API_KEY })\n```\n\n- **Isolation:** a managed [Sprites](https://sprites.dev) stateful sandbox\n  (Fly.io), a remote VM you do not run yourself.\n- **Auth / env:** needs `SPRITES_API_KEY` (token form\n  `org/projectNumber/tokenId/secret`); override the control-plane URL with\n  `apiUrl` / `SPRITES_API_URL`. Harness credentials are injected as workspace\n  secrets.\n- **Snapshot / resume:** resume-by-id reconnects to the named Sprite (its\n  filesystem is durable across idle suspend/resume). `snapshot()` creates a\n  Sprite **checkpoint** (a save point of the writable overlay); restore is\n  **in-place** on the same Sprite via the handle's `restoreCheckpoint()` /\n  `listCheckpoints()`. A checkpoint does not survive Sprite deletion, so the\n  provider intentionally does **not** implement the reconstruct-after-gone\n  `restoreSnapshot`, when a Sprite is gone the framework degrades to a fresh\n  create instead. Restore restarts the environment and can take minutes;\n  `restoreCheckpoint()` polls the workspace until it is listable again before\n  resolving. Note that immediately after a restore the overlay can be listable\n  while individual file reads briefly return an I/O error as it settles, so retry\n  reads if you act on the filesystem the instant restore returns.\n- **Ports:** a Sprite proxies a single internal HTTP port (default `8080`,\n  configurable via `httpPort`) to its always-on public URL. `ports.connect(8080)`\n  switches the URL to `public` auth and returns it; other ports are not exposed.\n- **Bridge:** like Daytona and Vercel, it is a remote VM, so bridged tools need the tunnel in\n  local dev (see [tools](./tools)).\n\n## Upstash Box\n\n```ts\nimport { upstashBoxSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-upstash-box'\n\nconst box = upstashBoxSandbox({ apiKey: process.env.UPSTASH_BOX_API_KEY })\n```\n\n- **Isolation:** a managed [Upstash Box](https://github.com/upstash/box) cloud\n  sandbox, a remote container you do not run yourself.\n- **Auth / env:** needs `UPSTASH_BOX_API_KEY` (or `apiKey`); override the API\n  base with `baseUrl` / `UPSTASH_BOX_BASE_URL`. Pick the image and size with\n  `runtime` (default `node`) and `size`.\n- **Paths:** the conventional `/workspace` virtual root maps to the box home,\n  `/workspace/home`, which is the handle's `workspaceRoot`.\n- **Processes:** `spawn()` opens a live `exec.session` over a WebSocket, so a\n  background process has a real in-box pid, a writable stdin, separate stdout and\n  stderr, and server-side signals. A session owns its process: dropping the\n  connection kills the command and sessions cannot be reattached, so `spawn()` is\n  scoped to the lifetime of the handle rather than the box. Blocking `exec()`\n  stays on the HTTP path and is shell-wrapped for `cwd`/env, which the session\n  takes natively.\n- **Snapshot / resume:** `snapshot()` calls `box.snapshot()` and\n  `restoreSnapshot()` reconstructs a new box from it via `Box.fromSnapshot()`, so\n  a snapshot survives deletion of the box that made it. Resume-by-id uses\n  `Box.get` (id or name) and probes `getStatus`, so a deleted record resumes as\n  `null` rather than a tombstone handle.\n- **Ports:** `ports.connect(port)` mints a preview URL via `getPublicURL`. Pass\n  `publicUrlAuth` to gate it, `{ bearerToken: true }` returns a token plus an\n  `Authorization: Bearer` header and `{ basicAuth: true }` returns Basic\n  credentials; without it the preview URL is unauthenticated.\n- **Network:** a `policy.capabilities.network` of `'deny'` maps to Box's\n  `deny-all` egress mode. The contract's gate is coarse, so Box's domain and CIDR\n  allowlists are not reachable through it. This is stricter than providers that\n  model deny as an allowlist: `deny-all` blocks every outbound connection, so an\n  agent that works under an allowlist-style deny will not reach package\n  registries or model provider hosts here. Leave the capability unset if the\n  agent needs either.\n- **Fork:** `fork()` snapshots the box and creates a new one from that snapshot,\n  the same shape as Docker's commit plus create. It costs a full snapshot round\n  trip (about 25 seconds), unlike Docker's local commit.\n- **Bridge:** like Daytona and Vercel, it is a remote VM, so bridged tools need\n  the tunnel in local dev (see [tools](./tools)).\n\n## Blaxel\n\n```ts\nimport { blaxelSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-blaxel'\n\nconst blaxel = blaxelSandbox({\n  apiKey: process.env.BL_API_KEY,\n  workspace: process.env.BL_WORKSPACE,\n})\n```\n\n- **Isolation:** a managed [Blaxel](https://blaxel.ai) cloud sandbox — a remote VM\n  you don't run yourself. Pick the image with `image` (default\n  `blaxel/base-image:latest`) and the size with `memory` (default 2048 MB). Set\n  `region` (or `BL_REGION`) to choose a region and to silence the SDK's warning\n  that it will become required.\n- **Auth / env:** Pass `apiKey` and `workspace` as constructor options, or set `BL_API_KEY` and `BL_WORKSPACE`. If you omit the API key, the provider uses the SDK CLI login or client credentials. A requested workspace must match those credentials. `@blaxel/core` authentication is process-global. The provider records the resolved workspace at construction and rejects a later provider that asks for a different workspace.\n- **Lifetime:** created sandboxes carry a `1h` TTL by default so an abandoned run\n  cannot strand a paid sandbox. Override with `ttl`, or pass `ttl: null` to manage\n  lifetime yourself.\n- **Resume:** resume-by-id reconnects to the named sandbox, and its filesystem\n  is durable across idle suspend/resume for the sandbox's lifetime. Blaxel's\n  snapshot/fork API is currently a source-scoped private preview, has no\n  entitlement probe, and does not document snapshots surviving source deletion.\n  The framework requires `snapshots` to reconstruct after the source is gone, so\n  this provider conservatively advertises both `snapshots` and `fork` as `false`\n  and does not expose `restoreSnapshot`.\n- **Ports:** `ports.connect(port)` creates a per-port preview URL. Previews are\n  token-gated by default and the returned channel carries both the token and the\n  ready-to-send `X-Blaxel-Preview-Token` header. Set `publicPreviews: true` for\n  unauthenticated URLs.\n- **Files:** `fs.watch()` is native, so file-event and diff hooks work without polling. `fs.lstat()` reports file, directory, and symlink metadata without following links; missing paths return `undefined`, while other errors propagate. Custom images must provide GNU `stat`.\n- **Process output:** stdout and stderr remain live-streamed through bounded\n  remote capture pipelines. Concurrent stdout and stderr use labeled records on\n  one transport stream, including across keepalive boundaries. Each stream has an 8 MiB total limit; exceeding it\n  fails and remotely reaps the process instead of accumulating unbounded logs in\n  the provider host. Cancellation uses the same process-group supervisor because\n  the pinned SDK does not prove named-process kill reaches child processes.\n  Custom images must provide Bash plus `cat`, `mkfifo`, `dd`, `base64`, `tr`,\n  and `wc` (the default Blaxel base image does). The supervisor invokes Bash\n  explicitly so job-control process groups do not depend on the image's\n  `/bin/sh` implementation.\n- **Resume semantics:** a destroyed sandbox does not disappear immediately —\n  Blaxel keeps the record in a teardown state before purging it. `resume()`\n  treats deleting, deactivating, failed, and terminated records as gone, while a\n  `DEACTIVATED` sandbox remains resumable consistently with the pinned SDK.\n- **Bridge:** like Daytona/Vercel, a remote VM — bridged tools need the tunnel in\n  local dev (see [tools](./tools)).\n\n## boxd\n\n```ts\nimport { boxdSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-boxd'\n\nconst boxd = boxdSandbox({\n  apiKey: process.env.BOXD_API_KEY,\n  org: 'acme',\n  vcpu: 2,\n})\n```\n\n- **Isolation:** a managed [boxd](https://boxd.sh) KVM microVM, a remote VM\n  you do not run yourself. Every machine is created `isolated`: no in-VM\n  `boxd` CLI, no metadata endpoint, no org integrations, and no peers on the\n  org network. The image is Ubuntu 24.04 with Node 24, Python 3, git, Docker,\n  and the Claude Code and Codex CLIs preinstalled.\n- **Auth / env:** needs `BOXD_API_KEY` (or `apiKey`) and the org the key\n  belongs to (`org` or `BOXD_ORG`). Override the endpoint with `baseUrl` /\n  `BOXD_BASE_URL`. Harness credentials are injected as workspace secrets and\n  travel as per-command env. The API key never enters the machine.\n- **Size:** pick `vcpu` (`1`, `2`, or `4`). boxd resolves memory from it:\n  4, 8, or 16 GiB. The default is the org's default size. Every machine has a\n  100 GB disk.\n- **Working directory:** the portable root `/workspace` maps to\n  `/home/boxd/workspace`. Override with `workdir`.\n- **Cancellation:** if you abort create or snapshot restore during startup,\n  the provider tries to delete the new machine after the current SDK call\n  finishes.\n- **Processes:** `spawn()` opens a streaming exec with separate stdout and\n  stderr and a writable stdin. `kill()` signals the process group inside the\n  machine and verifies that it is gone, so `killableProcesses` is measured,\n  not assumed.\n- **Snapshot / resume:** `snapshot()` captures memory and disk into a boxd\n  snapshot named `<machine>-<label>` and waits until it is restorable (about\n  25 s for an 8 GiB machine). `restoreSnapshot()` boots a new machine from it\n  in about 1 s, with the captured processes still running. Resume-by-id\n  reconnects to the same machine across stop (2 to 3 s to start), suspend\n  (about 140 ms) and hibernate.\n- **Fork:** `fork()` is a live boxd fork: disk, memory and running processes,\n  ready in under a second.\n- **Lifetime:** a machine is persistent until `destroy()`. It suspends after\n  `autoSuspendTimeout` idle seconds and hibernates after 4 hours idle by\n  default, at no compute cost, and wakes on the next command. Idle means no\n  inbound connection. Set `autoDestroyTimeout` as a safety net for abandoned\n  sandboxes. `stop` is a power-off: a file written seconds before it can still\n  sit in the page cache and be lost, so run `sync` before you stop a machine\n  yourself. Suspend, hibernate, snapshot and fork keep memory, so they do not\n  lose it.\n- **Ports:** every machine has one public HTTPS URL, `https://<name>.boxd.sh`.\n  `ports.connect(port)` pins that URL to `port` and returns it. The URL is\n  public: anyone who has it can reach the port.\n- **Golden image:** pass `fromSnapshot` to boot every new sandbox from a\n  snapshot you baked after `setup`, instead of the default image. The size is\n  then fixed by the snapshot.\n- **Bridge:** like the other cloud providers, it is a remote VM, so bridged\n  tools need the tunnel in local dev (see [tools](./tools)).\n\n## E2B\n\n```ts\nimport { e2bSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-e2b'\n\nconst e2b = e2bSandbox({ apiKey: process.env.E2B_API_KEY })\n```\n\n- **Isolation:** a managed [E2B](https://e2b.dev) sandbox, a Firecracker microVM\n  you do not run yourself. Pick the image with `template` (default: the E2B\n  `base` template).\n- **Auth / env:** needs `E2B_API_KEY` (or `apiKey`). Set `domain` (or\n  `E2B_DOMAIN`) for a self-hosted or BYOC deployment. Harness credentials are\n  injected as [workspace secrets](./provisioning). At create and snapshot\n  restore they are sent as sandbox `envs`. Per-command `env` goes through the\n  SDK's native `envs` argument. No value is written into a command string.\n- **Lifetime:** a sandbox lives for `timeoutMs` (default 30 minutes) and is\n  killed when that time elapses, so an abandoned run cannot keep billing.\n  Resume extends the lifetime by the same amount. Set `onTimeout: 'pause'` to\n  keep a timed-out sandbox resumable instead. E2B caps the lifetime at 1 hour\n  on the Hobby plan and 24 hours on Pro.\n- **Snapshot / resume:** `snapshot()` calls `createSnapshot()`. The sandbox is\n  paused briefly, then resumed. `restoreSnapshot()` creates a new sandbox from\n  that snapshot, so a snapshot survives deletion of its source. Resume-by-id\n  uses `Sandbox.connect`, which also wakes a paused sandbox. A killed or\n  expired sandbox resumes as `null`.\n- **Fork:** `fork()` is native. The parent is checkpointed in place and the copy\n  boots from that checkpoint.\n- **Processes:** every command runs as the leader of its own process group\n  (`setsid`), so `kill()` reaches backgrounded children. `kill()` always sends\n  `SIGKILL`. `spawn()` has a real sandbox pid, a writable stdin, and separate\n  stdout and stderr. A custom template must include `setsid` (util-linux); the\n  default template has it.\n- **Ports:** `ports.connect(port)` returns\n  `https://<port>-<sandbox-id>.<domain>`. With `allowPublicTraffic: false` the\n  URL is gated by the `e2b-traffic-access-token` header, and the channel carries\n  that header in `headers`. Browsers cannot send it, so leave public traffic on\n  for preview links a person clicks.\n- **Network:** `policy.capabilities.network: 'deny'` maps to\n  `allowInternetAccess: false`, which blocks all outbound traffic. E2B's\n  per-host allow and deny lists are not reachable through the contract's coarse\n  gate.\n- **Paths:** the portable root `/workspace` maps to `/home/user/workspace` by\n  default. Override with `workdir`. The sandbox user is `user` (not root) with\n  passwordless `sudo`, and `/workspace` itself is not writable.\n- **Bridge:** like Daytona and Vercel, it is a remote VM, so bridged tools need\n  the tunnel in local dev (see [tools](./tools)).\n\n## Railway\n\n```ts\nimport { railwaySandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-railway'\n\nconst railway = railwaySandbox({\n  environmentId: process.env.RAILWAY_ENVIRONMENT_ID,\n  region: 'us-west2',\n  idleTimeoutMinutes: 30,\n  resources: { cpu: 2, memoryGB: 4 },\n  // Optional: publish preview domains. Requires PRIVATE networking.\n  networkIsolation: 'PRIVATE',\n  ports: [3000],\n})\n```\n\n- **Isolation:** a managed [Railway](https://railway.com) sandbox, a VM you do\n  not run yourself, created in a Railway environment. Boot from the base image,\n  a named `checkpoint`, or a `Sandbox.template()` recipe (`template`).\n- **Auth / env:** the SDK reads `RAILWAY_TOKEN` (a project token) or\n  `RAILWAY_API_TOKEN` (an account or workspace token). An explicit `token`\n  defaults to bearer auth; pass `authType: 'project-token'` for a project\n  token. Scope is the environment (`environmentId` or\n  `RAILWAY_ENVIRONMENT_ID`), frozen per provider. Harness credentials are\n  injected as [workspace secrets](./provisioning): at create and restore they\n  are sent as the sandbox's runtime `env`, and per-command `env` travels in the\n  exec init frame. No value is written into a command string.\n- **Private networking:** with `networkIsolation: 'PRIVATE'` the sandbox joins\n  the environment's private network, so the agent can reach your other Railway\n  services (databases, internal APIs) by their private hostnames. The default,\n  `ISOLATED`, has no private-network access.\n- **Lifetime:** Railway destroys a sandbox after `idleTimeoutMinutes` of\n  inactivity (plan default when omitted; `0` disables idle destruction where\n  the plan allows it). There is no stop/start that keeps the disk, so\n  `durableFilesystem` is `false`: use a checkpoint or\n  [Portable Snapshots](./portable-snapshots) to keep work across idle expiry.\n  `SandboxCreateInput` carries no lifecycle hint, so set the timeout here.\n  Resume resets the idle countdown.\n- **Size:** `resources: { cpu, memoryGB }` sets vCPU (fractions allowed) and\n  memory in decimal GB for every create, restore, and fork. Omitted fields use\n  the workspace default; values above its maximum fail at create.\n- **Snapshot / resume:** `snapshot(label)` captures a native Railway checkpoint\n  named `tsai-<sandbox-id>-<uuid>`. The label stays on the returned ref only,\n  because checkpoint names are environment-scoped and a capture replaces an\n  existing name. Checkpoints belong to the environment, not the sandbox, so\n  `restoreSnapshot()` boots a new sandbox from one after its source is\n  destroyed, and reapplies env, network mode, domains, and idle timeout from\n  the provider config. Restores and forks run in the source region, so\n  `region` applies to fresh sandboxes only. Checkpoints count\n  against a per-plan quota and the contract has no delete hook: prune\n  `tsai-*` checkpoints you no longer reference with `Sandbox.checkpoints()`\n  and `Sandbox.deleteCheckpoint()` from the `railway` SDK. Resume-by-id uses\n  `Sandbox.connect`. A missing, `DESTROYED`, `DESTROYING`, or `FAILED` sandbox\n  resumes as `null`, a `CREATING` one is awaited, and auth or transport errors\n  are rethrown.\n- **Fork:** `fork()` is a native live disk fork of the running sandbox into a\n  new one in the same environment and region. Processes are not copied. The\n  fork gets the parent's network mode, domains, idle timeout, and a copy of\n  its env overlay.\n- **Processes:** stdout and stderr stay separate. `spawn()` runs in a durable\n  exec session with a flow-controlled stdin writer, so it keeps running if the\n  host disconnects; this provider does not expose reattach, because TanStack's\n  [run journal](./journal) owns run identity. Blocking `exec` runs without a\n  durable session and is killed if the connection drops. `spawn()` reports\n  `pid: -1` because the exec bridge exposes no remote pid. `kill()` and an\n  aborted `exec` send `TERM` to the process group and escalate to `KILL` after\n  2 s. An aborted `spawn` sends `KILL` at once. All of them settle on the\n  confirmed remote exit. An aborted `exec` resolves with the signalled exit\n  code.\n- **Cancellation:** if create fails or times out after Railway minted the\n  sandbox, or the caller aborts while it boots, the provider tries to destroy\n  it. Both are best effort.\n- **Ports:** Railway publishes HTTP domains at create time only. Declare them\n  with `ports` (at most 10), which requires `networkIsolation: 'PRIVATE'`;\n  `ports.connect(port)` returns `https://<domain>` for a declared port and\n  rejects any other. Without `ports`, the `ports` capability is `false`. The\n  domain is public: it carries no preview token.\n- **Network:** `networkPolicy` is `false`. Both modes keep public internet\n  egress, so `policy.capabilities.network: 'deny'` is rejected at create\n  instead of being dropped.\n- **Paths:** the portable root `/workspace` is a real directory created at\n  boot. Override with `workdir`. `fs.remove` is recursive (`rm -rf`) and\n  `fs.lstat` is a GNU `stat` probe that does not follow symlinks.\n- **Bridge:** like the other cloud providers, it is a remote VM, so bridged\n  tools need the tunnel in local dev (see [tools](./tools)).\n\n## Capabilities\n\nProviders declare what they support via `capabilities()`. The flags are:\n\n| Capability | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| `fs` | Read/write the sandbox filesystem. |\n| `exec` | Run commands. |\n| `env` | Inject environment variables. |\n| `ports` | Expose/forward ports (preview URLs). |\n| `backgroundProcesses` | Keep long-running processes alive between calls. |\n| `writableStdin` | A spawned process exposes a writable host→process stdin. `true` for local-process, Docker container, Daytona, Upstash Box, E2B, boxd, and Railway. `false` for Docker Sandboxes (`sbx`), Vercel, Sprites, Blaxel, and Cloudflare. When `false`, stdin-fed harnesses write the prompt to a file and redirect it in the shell. |\n| `killableProcesses` | A spawned process can be forcibly stopped via `SpawnHandle.kill()` **and** aborted mid-flight via the `signal` passed to `spawn`. |\n| `snapshots` | Capture and restore point-in-time snapshots. |\n| `networkPolicy` | Enforce network allow/deny rules. |\n| `durableFilesystem` | Disk that survives across resumes. |\n| `fork` | Branch a sandbox from an existing one. |\n\nCode that uses an **optional** capability checks the flag first and degrades\ngracefully. For example, bootstrap only snapshots when `snapshots` is supported,\nso `localProcessSandbox` simply skips the step. Calling an unsupported optional\nmethod directly (instead of checking the flag) throws an\n`UnsupportedCapabilityError`:\n\n```ts\nimport { localProcessSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-local-process'\n\nconst provider = localProcessSandbox()\nconst caps = provider.capabilities()\n\nif (caps.snapshots) {\n  // safe to take a snapshot\n} else {\n  // degrade gracefully, local-process has no snapshots\n}\n```\n\nUse the flags to write provider-agnostic code: branch on the capability rather\nthan the concrete provider, and your sandbox definition keeps working when you\nswap one provider for another.\n\n### `killableProcesses` across the bundled providers\n\nThis flag is **measured, not asserted**. A wrong `true` hands the journal reader\nan unstoppable `tail -f` and leaks a process per run, so a provider only declares\nit once killing has been observed to work against a real sandbox. Two of these\ndeclarations were once `true` on reasoning alone and both turned out to be false\nwhen probed (Docker's stream destroy left the container-side process in `ps`;\nlocal-process's `sh -c` did not `exec`, so killing the shell left the command\nalive). Anything that cannot be measured yet stays `false`, because `poll` is\nmerely slower while a wrong `follow` is a leak.\n\n| Provider | `killableProcesses` | Why |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Local process | `true` | **Measured.** Kills the process GROUP, not the wrapper: `detached` spawn plus `process.kill(-pid, signal)` on POSIX (killing only the `sh` left the command running, dash does not reliably `exec`); on Windows `taskkill /T` plus a verified sweep, see [Windows teardown](#windows-process-teardown-logger). |\n| Docker | `true` | **Measured.** Signals the process INSIDE the container by the pid the wrapper recorded for itself, process group first and escalating to `KILL`. Destroying the hijacked exec stream is *not* sufficient: it only detaches the client. |\n| Docker Sandboxes (`sbx`) | `false` | Unmeasured. Live tests named-skip until `sbx login`. The handle records a pid in the VM, but `killableProcesses` stays `false` until that kill is observed. |\n| Daytona | `false` | `kill()` only aborts the client-side poll loop and does not await any termination; the `deleteSession` that might terminate the command runs later from the pump's teardown, is failure-swallowed, and is documented as cleanup for a *completed* session. Unmeasured, needs `DAYTONA_API_KEY`. |\n| Vercel | `false` | The abort signal reaches only the HTTP request that STARTS a detached command, so the old `kill()` was a no-op. It now issues the SDK's server-side `Command.kill`, but whether that reaches a forked child (the follow command is a multi-statement shell, so `tail -f` is always a child) is unmeasured, needs Vercel credentials. |\n| Sprites | `true` (unverified) | Not a client-side detach: `kill()` issues a real server-side `POST /exec/<sessionId>/kill` before closing the socket. What that endpoint signals (process group or pid) is undocumented and unmeasured; needs `SPRITES_API_KEY`. |\n| Upstash Box | `true` | **Measured.** `kill()` sends an allowlisted signal (`TERM`/`KILL`/`INT`/`HUP`) that the box agent delivers to the process TREE server-side, so a forked child is signalled too. Verified against production: a spawned `sleep 5 && touch <marker>` was killed and the marker never appeared. Needs `UPSTASH_BOX_API_KEY`. |\n| Blaxel | `true` | The provider reaps supervisor and child process groups, then waits for the remote reaper to finish. Credential-gated journal conformance verifies that cancellation leaves no active follower process. |\n| E2B | `true` | **Measured.** The SDK's own kill is a SIGKILL to the shell pid, and a backgrounded `( … ) & wait` child survived it. Every command therefore runs as a `setsid` group leader and `kill()` runs `kill -KILL -- -<pid>` inside the sandbox. The shared journal conformance kill case passes against a real sandbox. Needs `E2B_API_KEY`. |\n| Cloudflare | `false` | `kill()` is a no-op, and the caller's `AbortSignal` reaches neither `exec` nor `spawn`, because Workers RPC cannot serialize one. |\n| boxd | `true` | **Measured.** The spawn wrapper runs under `setsid`, so the pid it records leads its own process group. `kill()` runs a shell inside the machine that signals that group, escalates to `KILL`, and checks with `kill -0`. Closing the stream alone is not a kill: the process survived it. Verified against production: a spawned `sleep 5 && touch <marker>` was killed and the marker never appeared. Needs `BOXD_API_KEY`. |\n| Railway | `true` | **Measured.** `kill()` and an aborted `exec` send `TERM` to the command's process group and escalate to `KILL` after 2 s. An aborted `spawn` sends `KILL` at once. Each settles only when the remote exit is confirmed. Verified against production: the shared journal conformance kill case passes, and spawned `sleep 5 && touch <marker>` commands (including a backgrounded child) were killed before the marker appeared, for `kill()`, an aborted `exec`, and an aborted `spawn`. Needs `RAILWAY_API_TOKEN` or `RAILWAY_TOKEN` + `RAILWAY_ENVIRONMENT_ID`. |\n\nEach of the remote providers registers the shared journal conformance suite, so\nthe claim is falsifiable rather than asserted: with credentials present the suite\nruns against a real sandbox, and without them it reports a **named skip** carrying\nthe reason instead of a silent pass. Cloudflare's gate is the runtime rather than\ncredentials, its provider can only create a sandbox through a `Sandbox` Durable\nObject binding, which no Node test process has, so its registration is a named\nskip saying exactly that, until a Workers-runtime harness can measure it.\n\nThis flag is required on every provider, including a bring-your-own one. A\nprovider that omitted it would be treated as killable, which is the dangerous\ndefault: a follower process started there could never be reclaimed, and it would\nkeep running inside the sandbox for as long as the sandbox lives.\n\nIt is the flag the [run journal](./journal) reads to decide how to tail a run's\noutput: a killable provider gets a streaming `tail -f`, and a provider like\nCloudflare gets a loop of bounded reads, each of which terminates on its own.\n\nThe flag also bounds what *cancel* can mean. On a `false` provider there is no\nsignal path to the agent process, so the only cancel that actually stops the\nagent means destroying the sandbox, which is what the cancel path does. See\n[what cancel means on a provider that cannot kill](./takeover#what-cancel-means-on-a-provider-that-cannot-kill).\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Providers\nid: providers\norder: 3\ndescription: \"Pick and configure where a TanStack AI sandbox runs (local process, Docker container, Docker Sandboxes microVM, Daytona, Vercel, Upstash Box, Blaxel, E2B, boxd, or Railway) and what each one can do.\"\n---\n\nA provider owns the isolation primitive: where the harness actually runs. Every\nprovider implements the same `SandboxProvider` / `SandboxHandle` contract, so the\n[workspace](./workspace) you hand the agent and the [policy](./policy) that guards\nit are provider-agnostic. Pick a provider for the isolation, auth, and\nsnapshot/resume behaviour you need; the rest of your sandbox definition stays the\nsame.\n\nProvider-native snapshots and resume keep or recreate provider state. They can\nreduce bootstrap time. [Portable Snapshots](./portable-snapshots) store\ncompleted workspace data in your application persistence for reconstruction.\n\n> The provider is _where_ the agent runs. For _which_ agent runs (Grok Build,\n> Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, or any ACP agent via `acpCompatible`) see\n> [Harnesses](./harnesses).\n\n## Choosing a provider\n\n| Provider | Package | Isolation | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Local process | `@tanstack/ai-sandbox-local-process` | none (host) | The fast, no-Docker dev loop. Trusted/dev use only. |\n| Docker | `@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker` | container | Real isolation; commit-based snapshots, fork, resume-by-id. |\n| Docker Sandboxes (`sbx`) | `@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker` | microVM | Native Docker Sandboxes. Same package as `dockerSandbox()`, different export: `sbxSandbox()`. Needs the `sbx` CLI, a login, and a hypervisor. |\n| Daytona | `@tanstack/ai-sandbox-daytona` | cloud sandbox | Managed [Daytona](https://www.daytona.io/) sandboxes; snapshots after setup, port preview links, resume-by-id. Needs `DAYTONA_API_KEY`. |\n| Vercel | `@tanstack/ai-sandbox-vercel` | microVM | Managed [Vercel Sandbox](https://vercel.com/docs/sandbox) microVMs; exposed-port domains, resume-by-id (persistent). Needs `VERCEL_TOKEN` + team/project. |\n| Sprites | `@tanstack/ai-sandbox-sprites` | stateful sandbox | Managed [Sprites](https://sprites.dev) (Fly.io) sandboxes; durable filesystem, in-place checkpoints, single proxied public-URL port, resume-by-id. Needs `SPRITES_API_KEY`. |\n| Upstash Box | `@tanstack/ai-sandbox-upstash-box` | cloud sandbox | Managed [Upstash Box](https://github.com/upstash/box) sandboxes; interactive processes over a WebSocket session (real pid, stdin, signals), native snapshots, preview URLs, resume-by-id. Needs `UPSTASH_BOX_API_KEY`. |\n| Blaxel | `@tanstack/ai-sandbox-blaxel` | cloud sandbox | Managed [Blaxel](https://blaxel.ai) sandboxes; durable filesystem, per-port preview URLs, native file watch, resume-by-id. Snapshot/fork remain disabled while the source-scoped private-preview semantics are unproven. Accepts `BL_API_KEY` + `BL_WORKSPACE` or SDK-resolved CLI or client credentials. |\n| E2B | `@tanstack/ai-sandbox-e2b` | microVM | Managed [E2B](https://e2b.dev) Firecracker sandboxes. Native snapshots and fork, preview URLs, writable stdin, process-group kill, resume-by-id (also wakes a paused sandbox). Needs `E2B_API_KEY`. |\n| boxd | `@tanstack/ai-sandbox-boxd` | microVM | Managed [boxd](https://boxd.sh) KVM microVMs; live fork (memory and processes), snapshots that restore into a new machine, persistent disk, resume-by-id across stop, suspend and hibernate, one public HTTPS URL per machine. Needs `BOXD_API_KEY` + `BOXD_ORG`. |\n| Railway | `@tanstack/ai-sandbox-railway` | microVM | Managed [Railway](https://railway.com) sandboxes. Private networking to your other Railway services, live disk fork, environment-scoped checkpoints that survive deletion of their source, durable exec sessions, create-time HTTP domains, resume-by-id. Needs `RAILWAY_API_TOKEN` or `RAILWAY_TOKEN` + an environment. |\n\nMost providers are their own package. `dockerSandbox()` and `sbxSandbox()` both\ncome from `@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker`. The constructor is the only thing that\ndiffers between them:\n\n```ts\nimport { localProcessSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-local-process'\nimport { dockerSandbox, sbxSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker'\nimport { daytonaSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-daytona'\nimport { vercelSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-vercel'\nimport { upstashBoxSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-upstash-box'\nimport { blaxelSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-blaxel'\nimport { e2bSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-e2b'\nimport { boxdSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-boxd'\nimport { railwaySandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-railway'\n\nconst dev = localProcessSandbox() // runs on your host\nconst isolated = dockerSandbox({ image: 'node:22' }) // container\nconst microvm = sbxSandbox() // Docker Sandboxes microVM\nconst daytona = daytonaSandbox({ apiKey: process.env.DAYTONA_API_KEY }) // managed cloud sandbox\nconst vercel = vercelSandbox({ runtime: 'node24' }) // managed Vercel microVM\nconst box = upstashBoxSandbox({ apiKey: process.env.UPSTASH_BOX_API_KEY }) // managed Upstash Box\nconst blaxel = blaxelSandbox() // managed Blaxel sandbox; uses API-key or CLI credentials\nconst e2b = e2bSandbox() // managed E2B microVM; reads E2B_API_KEY\nconst boxd = boxdSandbox({ org: 'acme' }) // managed boxd microVM; reads BOXD_API_KEY\nconst railway = railwaySandbox() // managed Railway sandbox; reads RAILWAY_TOKEN + RAILWAY_ENVIRONMENT_ID\n```\n\n> Cloud providers (including Daytona, Vercel, Sprites, Upstash Box, Blaxel, E2B, boxd, and Railway)\n> run remotely. When you drive them from your laptop, [tools](./tools) bridged\n> from `chat()` can't dial your machine's\n> `localhost`, you need the bridge tunnel. See the [tools guide](./tools) for the\n> ngrok subpath, and the [Cloudflare guide](./cloudflare) for the edge-native\n> co-located model.\n\n## Local process\n\n```ts\nimport { localProcessSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-local-process'\n\nconst dev = localProcessSandbox()\n```\n\n- **Isolation:** none. The harness runs directly on your host, inheriting your\n  host environment. Use it for trusted or dev work only. There is no boundary\n  between the agent and your machine.\n- **Auth / env:** inherits the host environment. Set `authMode` on the harness\n  (`'host'` or `'api-key'`). The provider does not pick this. See\n  [Harness Auth](./auth).\n- **Snapshot / resume:** no snapshots and no durable resume-by-id; each run\n  re-creates and re-bootstraps under the same identity. The snapshot step is\n  skipped silently (see [Capabilities](#capabilities)).\n\n### Host login vs API key (`scrubEnv`)\n\nThe provider is where the agent runs, not how it signs in. The default\n`authMode` is `'api-key'`. Set `'host'` when the machine already has a CLI\nlogin. A local-process run can be your laptop or a GitHub runner. See\n[Harness Auth](./auth).\n\n`localProcessSandbox` inherits the host environment, including any API keys\nexported there. If you set `authMode: 'host'`, pass `scrubEnv` so those keys\ndo not override the CLI login:\n\n```ts\nimport { localProcessSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-local-process'\n\nconst hostLogin = localProcessSandbox({\n  scrubEnv: ['XAI_API_KEY', 'GROK_API_KEY'],\n})\n```\n\nIf the same local-process sandbox runs on a CI machine, set\n`authMode: 'api-key'`. Then inject the key as a workspace secret. Isolated and\ncloud providers have no host CLI login. Use `authMode: 'api-key'` and\nworkspace secrets there.\n\n### Windows process teardown (`logger`)\n\nKilling a spawned process means killing the whole tree, and on Windows that takes\nmore than `taskkill /T`. Commands run through a git-bash `sh`, and MSYS's\nfork emulation runs the final command of a statement list, such as the\n`tail -f` behind a [journal](./journal) follow read, under an intermediate shell\nthat immediately exits. Windows never reparents, so the surviving process points\nat a dead parent and `taskkill /T`, which walks only live parent links, cannot\nreach it **while still exiting `0`**. Left alone, every follow read leaks a\nprocess for the life of the machine.\n\n`localProcessSandbox` therefore consults MSYS's own process table, which does\nkeep the logical parentage before killing, then kills any descendant `/T` missed.\nTeardown is total by construction: it never throws, because a throwing kill would\nstrand a run mid-flight. That means a kill it genuinely cannot complete (a\nprotected process, access denied) is otherwise invisible, so pass a `logger` to\nsee it:\n\n```ts\nimport { localProcessSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-local-process'\n\nconst dev = localProcessSandbox({\n  logger: {\n    warn: (message, meta) => console.warn(message, meta),\n  },\n})\n```\n\nAny object with a `warn(message, meta?)` method works, so the `InternalLogger`\nyour adapter already receives can be handed straight in. A process that had\nalready exited on its own is **not** a failure and is never reported.\n\nNothing here changes on POSIX, where `sh` really is the command's parent and\nsignalling the wrapper is enough.\n\n## Docker\n\n```ts\nimport { dockerSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker'\n\nconst isolated = dockerSandbox({ image: 'node:22' })\n```\n\n- **Isolation:** a real container boundary between the agent and your host.\n- **Auth / env:** no host login; provide credentials as workspace secrets, which\n  are injected into the container env at create/resume. The agent reaches host\n  tools over `host.docker.internal` (see [tools](./tools)).\n- **Snapshot / resume:** full commit-based snapshots, `fork`, and resume-by-id.\n  Bootstrap snapshots after `setup` completes, so subsequent runs resume from the\n  snapshot instead of re-running setup.\n\n## Docker Sandboxes (sbx)\n\n```ts\nimport { sbxSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker'\nimport { defineSandbox, defineWorkspace, githubRepo } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n\nconst isolated = sbxSandbox({\n  allowNetwork: ['*.npmjs.org', 'registry.npmjs.org'],\n})\n\nconst sandbox = defineSandbox({\n  id: 'repo-agent',\n  provider: isolated,\n  workspace: defineWorkspace({\n    source: githubRepo({ repo: 'owner/repo' }),\n    setup: ['pnpm install'],\n  }),\n})\n```\n\n- **Isolation:** a hypervisor microVM. The sandbox has its own kernel and its own Docker daemon. This is not `dockerSandbox()`, which starts a container.\n- **Needs:** `sbx` on `PATH`, `sbx login` (or a PAT piped to `sbx login --password-stdin` in CI), and a hypervisor (Hyper-V, Virtualization.framework, or KVM). A Docker socket is not enough.\n- **Workspace:** `sbx create --clone` copies a host Git repo into the VM. Pass `workspaceDir` that contains `.git`, or set `workspace.source` to a git URL. If there is no Git repo, create throws. There is no bind-mount fallback.\n- **Auth / env:** inject API keys as workspace secrets. v1 does not call `sbx secret`.\n- **Snapshot / resume:** no snapshots and no fork. Resume reconnects by name (`sbx ls`). A stopped sandbox starts again on the next `sbx exec`.\n- **Network:** this is the first provider with `networkPolicy: true`. See [Policy](./policy).\n\n## Daytona\n\n```ts\nimport { daytonaSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-daytona'\n\nconst daytona = daytonaSandbox({\n  apiKey: process.env.DAYTONA_API_KEY,\n  snapshot: 'daytona-medium',\n  autoStopInterval: 0,\n})\n```\n\n- **Isolation:** a managed cloud sandbox on a remote VM you do not run yourself.\n- **Auth / env:** needs `DAYTONA_API_KEY`. Put harness credentials in\n  [workspace secrets](./provisioning). At create and snapshot restore,\n  Daytona stores each value as an organization Secret and mounts a\n  placeholder in the sandbox env. The create record, the dashboard env\n  view, and session command strings do not contain the real value. Daytona\n  substitutes the value on outbound HTTPS requests. Per-command `opts.env`\n  uses `executeCommand`'s env argument or a sourced env file. It never\n  writes `export KEY=` prefixes into the command string.\n- **Snapshot / resume:** point-in-time snapshots after setup (default when\n  `lifecycle.snapshot` is `'after-setup'`). Pass `snapshot` on\n  `daytonaSandbox()` to pick the Daytona image (for example\n  `'daytona-medium'`). Resume starts a `stopped` or `archived` sandbox, then\n  returns the handle.\n- **Idle stop:** Daytona stops an idle sandbox after 15 minutes by default.\n  Set `autoStopInterval` in minutes to change that. Pass `0` to turn auto-stop\n  off. Set `ephemeral: true` to delete the sandbox when it stops.\n- **Network:** `policy.capabilities.network: 'deny'` blocks all outbound\n  network on create.\n- **Working directory:** the portable root `/workspace` maps to\n  `/home/daytona/workspace` by default. Override with `workdir` on\n  `daytonaSandbox()` if you need another path.\n- **Stdin:** spawned processes accept host stdin (`writableStdin: true`).\n- **Privileges:** the Daytona user is not root. Package installs in `setup`\n  must use `sudo -n` (for example `sudo -n apt-get install …`). Do not put\n  `sudo *` in a [policy](./policy) deny list for this provider.\n- **Bridge:** the sandbox is remote, so a [bridged tool](./tools) call cannot\n  reach your laptop's `localhost`. In local dev, tunnel the bridge (see\n  [tools](./tools)). A deployed orchestrator is reachable without a tunnel.\n\nDefault headless path on Daytona:\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { grokBuildText } from '@tanstack/ai-grok-build'\nimport {\n  defineSandbox,\n  defineSandboxPolicy,\n  defineWorkspace,\n  gitSkill,\n  githubRepo,\n  withSandbox,\n} from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { daytonaSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-daytona'\n\nconst sandbox = defineSandbox({\n  id: 'daytona-agent',\n  provider: daytonaSandbox({\n    apiKey: process.env.DAYTONA_API_KEY,\n    snapshot: 'daytona-medium',\n  }),\n  workspace: defineWorkspace({\n    source: githubRepo({ repo: 'owner/app' }),\n    skills: [gitSkill({ repo: 'owner/skills-pack' })],\n  }),\n  policy: defineSandboxPolicy({\n    default: 'allow',\n  }),\n})\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: grokBuildText('grok-build'),\n  messages: [{ role: 'user', content: 'List the project files.' }],\n  middleware: [withSandbox(sandbox)],\n})\n```\n\nHeadless Grok Build and Codex stay on auto-approve with `default: 'allow'`.\nIsolation is the Daytona VM. Use Claude Code when you need command-level deny.\n\n## Vercel\n\n```ts\nimport { vercelSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-vercel'\n\nconst vercel = vercelSandbox({ runtime: 'node24' })\n```\n\n- **Isolation:** a managed microVM (Vercel Sandbox).\n- **Auth / env:** needs `VERCEL_TOKEN` plus a team/project. Harness credentials\n  are injected as workspace secrets.\n- **Snapshot / resume:** persistent resume-by-id with a durable filesystem, plus\n  exposed-port domains for previews.\n- **Bridge:** like Daytona, it is a remote VM, so bridged tools need the tunnel in local\n  dev (see [tools](./tools)).\n\n## Sprites\n\n```ts\nimport { spritesSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-sprites'\n\nconst sprites = spritesSandbox({ apiKey: process.env.SPRITES_API_KEY })\n```\n\n- **Isolation:** a managed [Sprites](https://sprites.dev) stateful sandbox\n  (Fly.io), a remote VM you do not run yourself.\n- **Auth / env:** needs `SPRITES_API_KEY` (token form\n  `org/projectNumber/tokenId/secret`); override the control-plane URL with\n  `apiUrl` / `SPRITES_API_URL`. Harness credentials are injected as workspace\n  secrets.\n- **Snapshot / resume:** resume-by-id reconnects to the named Sprite (its\n  filesystem is durable across idle suspend/resume). `snapshot()` creates a\n  Sprite **checkpoint** (a save point of the writable overlay); restore is\n  **in-place** on the same Sprite via the handle's `restoreCheckpoint()` /\n  `listCheckpoints()`. A checkpoint does not survive Sprite deletion, so the\n  provider intentionally does **not** implement the reconstruct-after-gone\n  `restoreSnapshot`, when a Sprite is gone the framework degrades to a fresh\n  create instead. Restore restarts the environment and can take minutes;\n  `restoreCheckpoint()` polls the workspace until it is listable again before\n  resolving. Note that immediately after a restore the overlay can be listable\n  while individual file reads briefly return an I/O error as it settles, so retry\n  reads if you act on the filesystem the instant restore returns.\n- **Ports:** a Sprite proxies a single internal HTTP port (default `8080`,\n  configurable via `httpPort`) to its always-on public URL. `ports.connect(8080)`\n  switches the URL to `public` auth and returns it; other ports are not exposed.\n- **Bridge:** like Daytona and Vercel, it is a remote VM, so bridged tools need the tunnel in\n  local dev (see [tools](./tools)).\n\n## Upstash Box\n\n```ts\nimport { upstashBoxSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-upstash-box'\n\nconst box = upstashBoxSandbox({ apiKey: process.env.UPSTASH_BOX_API_KEY })\n```\n\n- **Isolation:** a managed [Upstash Box](https://github.com/upstash/box) cloud\n  sandbox, a remote container you do not run yourself.\n- **Auth / env:** needs `UPSTASH_BOX_API_KEY` (or `apiKey`); override the API\n  base with `baseUrl` / `UPSTASH_BOX_BASE_URL`. Pick the image and size with\n  `runtime` (default `node`) and `size`.\n- **Paths:** the conventional `/workspace` virtual root maps to the box home,\n  `/workspace/home`, which is the handle's `workspaceRoot`.\n- **Processes:** `spawn()` opens a live `exec.session` over a WebSocket, so a\n  background process has a real in-box pid, a writable stdin, separate stdout and\n  stderr, and server-side signals. A session owns its process: dropping the\n  connection kills the command and sessions cannot be reattached, so `spawn()` is\n  scoped to the lifetime of the handle rather than the box. Blocking `exec()`\n  stays on the HTTP path and is shell-wrapped for `cwd`/env, which the session\n  takes natively.\n- **Snapshot / resume:** `snapshot()` calls `box.snapshot()` and\n  `restoreSnapshot()` reconstructs a new box from it via `Box.fromSnapshot()`, so\n  a snapshot survives deletion of the box that made it. Resume-by-id uses\n  `Box.get` (id or name) and probes `getStatus`, so a deleted record resumes as\n  `null` rather than a tombstone handle.\n- **Ports:** `ports.connect(port)` mints a preview URL via `getPublicURL`. Pass\n  `publicUrlAuth` to gate it, `{ bearerToken: true }` returns a token plus an\n  `Authorization: Bearer` header and `{ basicAuth: true }` returns Basic\n  credentials; without it the preview URL is unauthenticated.\n- **Network:** a `policy.capabilities.network` of `'deny'` maps to Box's\n  `deny-all` egress mode. The contract's gate is coarse, so Box's domain and CIDR\n  allowlists are not reachable through it. This is stricter than providers that\n  model deny as an allowlist: `deny-all` blocks every outbound connection, so an\n  agent that works under an allowlist-style deny will not reach package\n  registries or model provider hosts here. Leave the capability unset if the\n  agent needs either.\n- **Fork:** `fork()` snapshots the box and creates a new one from that snapshot,\n  the same shape as Docker's commit plus create. It costs a full snapshot round\n  trip (about 25 seconds), unlike Docker's local commit.\n- **Bridge:** like Daytona and Vercel, it is a remote VM, so bridged tools need\n  the tunnel in local dev (see [tools](./tools)).\n\n## Blaxel\n\n```ts\nimport { blaxelSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-blaxel'\n\nconst blaxel = blaxelSandbox({\n  apiKey: process.env.BL_API_KEY,\n  workspace: process.env.BL_WORKSPACE,\n})\n```\n\n- **Isolation:** a managed [Blaxel](https://blaxel.ai) cloud sandbox — a remote VM\n  you don't run yourself. Pick the image with `image` (default\n  `blaxel/base-image:latest`) and the size with `memory` (default 2048 MB). Set\n  `region` (or `BL_REGION`) to choose a region and to silence the SDK's warning\n  that it will become required.\n- **Auth / env:** Pass `apiKey` and `workspace` as constructor options, or set `BL_API_KEY` and `BL_WORKSPACE`. If you omit the API key, the provider uses the SDK CLI login or client credentials. A requested workspace must match those credentials. `@blaxel/core` authentication is process-global. The provider records the resolved workspace at construction and rejects a later provider that asks for a different workspace.\n- **Lifetime:** created sandboxes carry a `1h` TTL by default so an abandoned run\n  cannot strand a paid sandbox. Override with `ttl`, or pass `ttl: null` to manage\n  lifetime yourself.\n- **Resume:** resume-by-id reconnects to the named sandbox, and its filesystem\n  is durable across idle suspend/resume for the sandbox's lifetime. Blaxel's\n  snapshot/fork API is currently a source-scoped private preview, has no\n  entitlement probe, and does not document snapshots surviving source deletion.\n  The framework requires `snapshots` to reconstruct after the source is gone, so\n  this provider conservatively advertises both `snapshots` and `fork` as `false`\n  and does not expose `restoreSnapshot`.\n- **Ports:** `ports.connect(port)` creates a per-port preview URL. Previews are\n  token-gated by default and the returned channel carries both the token and the\n  ready-to-send `X-Blaxel-Preview-Token` header. Set `publicPreviews: true` for\n  unauthenticated URLs.\n- **Files:** `fs.watch()` is native, so file-event and diff hooks work without polling. `fs.lstat()` reports file, directory, and symlink metadata without following links; missing paths return `undefined`, while other errors propagate. Custom images must provide GNU `stat`.\n- **Process output:** stdout and stderr remain live-streamed through bounded\n  remote capture pipelines. Concurrent stdout and stderr use labeled records on\n  one transport stream, including across keepalive boundaries. Each stream has an 8 MiB total limit; exceeding it\n  fails and remotely reaps the process instead of accumulating unbounded logs in\n  the provider host. Cancellation uses the same process-group supervisor because\n  the pinned SDK does not prove named-process kill reaches child processes.\n  Custom images must provide Bash plus `cat`, `mkfifo`, `dd`, `base64`, `tr`,\n  and `wc` (the default Blaxel base image does). The supervisor invokes Bash\n  explicitly so job-control process groups do not depend on the image's\n  `/bin/sh` implementation.\n- **Resume semantics:** a destroyed sandbox does not disappear immediately —\n  Blaxel keeps the record in a teardown state before purging it. `resume()`\n  treats deleting, deactivating, failed, and terminated records as gone, while a\n  `DEACTIVATED` sandbox remains resumable consistently with the pinned SDK.\n- **Bridge:** like Daytona/Vercel, a remote VM — bridged tools need the tunnel in\n  local dev (see [tools](./tools)).\n\n## boxd\n\n```ts\nimport { boxdSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-boxd'\n\nconst boxd = boxdSandbox({\n  apiKey: process.env.BOXD_API_KEY,\n  org: 'acme',\n  vcpu: 2,\n})\n```\n\n- **Isolation:** a managed [boxd](https://boxd.sh) KVM microVM, a remote VM\n  you do not run yourself. Every machine is created `isolated`: no in-VM\n  `boxd` CLI, no metadata endpoint, no org integrations, and no peers on the\n  org network. The image is Ubuntu 24.04 with Node 24, Python 3, git, Docker,\n  and the Claude Code and Codex CLIs preinstalled.\n- **Auth / env:** needs `BOXD_API_KEY` (or `apiKey`) and the org the key\n  belongs to (`org` or `BOXD_ORG`). Override the endpoint with `baseUrl` /\n  `BOXD_BASE_URL`. Harness credentials are injected as workspace secrets and\n  travel as per-command env. The API key never enters the machine.\n- **Size:** pick `vcpu` (`1`, `2`, or `4`). boxd resolves memory from it:\n  4, 8, or 16 GiB. The default is the org's default size. Every machine has a\n  100 GB disk.\n- **Working directory:** the portable root `/workspace` maps to\n  `/home/boxd/workspace`. Override with `workdir`.\n- **Cancellation:** if you abort create or snapshot restore during startup,\n  the provider tries to delete the new machine after the current SDK call\n  finishes.\n- **Processes:** `spawn()` opens a streaming exec with separate stdout and\n  stderr and a writable stdin. `kill()` signals the process group inside the\n  machine and verifies that it is gone, so `killableProcesses` is measured,\n  not assumed.\n- **Snapshot / resume:** `snapshot()` captures memory and disk into a boxd\n  snapshot named `<machine>-<label>` and waits until it is restorable (about\n  25 s for an 8 GiB machine). `restoreSnapshot()` boots a new machine from it\n  in about 1 s, with the captured processes still running. Resume-by-id\n  reconnects to the same machine across stop (2 to 3 s to start), suspend\n  (about 140 ms) and hibernate.\n- **Fork:** `fork()` is a live boxd fork: disk, memory and running processes,\n  ready in under a second.\n- **Lifetime:** a machine is persistent until `destroy()`. It suspends after\n  `autoSuspendTimeout` idle seconds and hibernates after 4 hours idle by\n  default, at no compute cost, and wakes on the next command. Idle means no\n  inbound connection. Set `autoDestroyTimeout` as a safety net for abandoned\n  sandboxes. `stop` is a power-off: a file written seconds before it can still\n  sit in the page cache and be lost, so run `sync` before you stop a machine\n  yourself. Suspend, hibernate, snapshot and fork keep memory, so they do not\n  lose it.\n- **Ports:** every machine has one public HTTPS URL, `https://<name>.boxd.sh`.\n  `ports.connect(port)` pins that URL to `port` and returns it. The URL is\n  public: anyone who has it can reach the port.\n- **Golden image:** pass `fromSnapshot` to boot every new sandbox from a\n  snapshot you baked after `setup`, instead of the default image. The size is\n  then fixed by the snapshot.\n- **Bridge:** like the other cloud providers, it is a remote VM, so bridged\n  tools need the tunnel in local dev (see [tools](./tools)).\n\n## E2B\n\n```ts\nimport { e2bSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-e2b'\n\nconst e2b = e2bSandbox({ apiKey: process.env.E2B_API_KEY })\n```\n\n- **Isolation:** a managed [E2B](https://e2b.dev) sandbox, a Firecracker microVM\n  you do not run yourself. Pick the image with `template` (default: the E2B\n  `base` template).\n- **Auth / env:** needs `E2B_API_KEY` (or `apiKey`). Set `domain` (or\n  `E2B_DOMAIN`) for a self-hosted or BYOC deployment. Harness credentials are\n  injected as [workspace secrets](./provisioning). At create and snapshot\n  restore they are sent as sandbox `envs`. Per-command `env` goes through the\n  SDK's native `envs` argument. No value is written into a command string.\n- **Lifetime:** a sandbox lives for `timeoutMs` (default 30 minutes) and is\n  killed when that time elapses, so an abandoned run cannot keep billing.\n  Resume extends the lifetime by the same amount. Set `onTimeout: 'pause'` to\n  keep a timed-out sandbox resumable instead. E2B caps the lifetime at 1 hour\n  on the Hobby plan and 24 hours on Pro.\n- **Snapshot / resume:** `snapshot()` calls `createSnapshot()`. The sandbox is\n  paused briefly, then resumed. `restoreSnapshot()` creates a new sandbox from\n  that snapshot, so a snapshot survives deletion of its source. Resume-by-id\n  uses `Sandbox.connect`, which also wakes a paused sandbox. A killed or\n  expired sandbox resumes as `null`.\n- **Fork:** `fork()` is native. The parent is checkpointed in place and the copy\n  boots from that checkpoint.\n- **Processes:** every command runs as the leader of its own process group\n  (`setsid`), so `kill()` reaches backgrounded children. `kill()` always sends\n  `SIGKILL`. `spawn()` has a real sandbox pid, a writable stdin, and separate\n  stdout and stderr. A custom template must include `setsid` (util-linux); the\n  default template has it.\n- **Ports:** `ports.connect(port)` returns\n  `https://<port>-<sandbox-id>.<domain>`. With `allowPublicTraffic: false` the\n  URL is gated by the `e2b-traffic-access-token` header, and the channel carries\n  that header in `headers`. Browsers cannot send it, so leave public traffic on\n  for preview links a person clicks.\n- **Network:** `policy.capabilities.network: 'deny'` maps to\n  `allowInternetAccess: false`, which blocks all outbound traffic. E2B's\n  per-host allow and deny lists are not reachable through the contract's coarse\n  gate.\n- **Paths:** the portable root `/workspace` maps to `/home/user/workspace` by\n  default. Override with `workdir`. The sandbox user is `user` (not root) with\n  passwordless `sudo`, and `/workspace` itself is not writable.\n- **Bridge:** like Daytona and Vercel, it is a remote VM, so bridged tools need\n  the tunnel in local dev (see [tools](./tools)).\n\n## Railway\n\n```ts\nimport { railwaySandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-railway'\n\nconst railway = railwaySandbox({\n  environmentId: process.env.RAILWAY_ENVIRONMENT_ID,\n  region: 'us-west2',\n  idleTimeoutMinutes: 30,\n  resources: { cpu: 2, memoryGB: 4 },\n  // Optional: publish preview domains. Requires PRIVATE networking.\n  networkIsolation: 'PRIVATE',\n  ports: [3000],\n})\n```\n\n- **Isolation:** a managed [Railway](https://railway.com) sandbox, a VM you do\n  not run yourself, created in a Railway environment. Boot from the base image,\n  a named `checkpoint`, or a `Sandbox.template()` recipe (`template`).\n- **Auth / env:** the SDK reads `RAILWAY_TOKEN` (a project token) or\n  `RAILWAY_API_TOKEN` (an account or workspace token). An explicit `token`\n  defaults to bearer auth; pass `authType: 'project-token'` for a project\n  token. Scope is the environment (`environmentId` or\n  `RAILWAY_ENVIRONMENT_ID`), frozen per provider. Harness credentials are\n  injected as [workspace secrets](./provisioning): at create and restore they\n  are sent as the sandbox's runtime `env`, and per-command `env` travels in the\n  exec init frame. No value is written into a command string.\n- **Private networking:** with `networkIsolation: 'PRIVATE'` the sandbox joins\n  the environment's private network, so the agent can reach your other Railway\n  services (databases, internal APIs) by their private hostnames. The default,\n  `ISOLATED`, has no private-network access.\n- **Lifetime:** Railway destroys a sandbox after `idleTimeoutMinutes` of\n  inactivity (plan default when omitted; `0` disables idle destruction where\n  the plan allows it). There is no stop/start that keeps the disk, so\n  `durableFilesystem` is `false`: use a checkpoint or\n  [Portable Snapshots](./portable-snapshots) to keep work across idle expiry.\n  `SandboxCreateInput` carries no lifecycle hint, so set the timeout here.\n  Resume resets the idle countdown.\n- **Size:** `resources: { cpu, memoryGB }` sets vCPU (fractions allowed) and\n  memory in decimal GB for every create, restore, and fork. Omitted fields use\n  the workspace default; values above its maximum fail at create.\n- **Snapshot / resume:** `snapshot(label)` captures a native Railway checkpoint\n  named `tsai-<sandbox-id>-<uuid>`. The label stays on the returned ref only,\n  because checkpoint names are environment-scoped and a capture replaces an\n  existing name. Checkpoints belong to the environment, not the sandbox, so\n  `restoreSnapshot()` boots a new sandbox from one after its source is\n  destroyed, and reapplies env, network mode, domains, and idle timeout from\n  the provider config. Restores and forks run in the source region, so\n  `region` applies to fresh sandboxes only. Checkpoints count\n  against a per-plan quota and the contract has no delete hook: prune\n  `tsai-*` checkpoints you no longer reference with `Sandbox.checkpoints()`\n  and `Sandbox.deleteCheckpoint()` from the `railway` SDK. Resume-by-id uses\n  `Sandbox.connect`. A missing, `DESTROYED`, `DESTROYING`, or `FAILED` sandbox\n  resumes as `null`, a `CREATING` one is awaited, and auth or transport errors\n  are rethrown.\n- **Fork:** `fork()` is a native live disk fork of the running sandbox into a\n  new one in the same environment and region. Processes are not copied. The\n  fork gets the parent's network mode, domains, idle timeout, and a copy of\n  its env overlay.\n- **Processes:** stdout and stderr stay separate. `spawn()` runs in a durable\n  exec session with a flow-controlled stdin writer, so it keeps running if the\n  host disconnects; this provider does not expose reattach, because TanStack's\n  [run journal](./journal) owns run identity. Blocking `exec` runs without a\n  durable session and is killed if the connection drops. `spawn()` reports\n  `pid: -1` because the exec bridge exposes no remote pid. `kill()` and an\n  aborted `exec` send `TERM` to the process group and escalate to `KILL` after\n  2 s. An aborted `spawn` sends `KILL` at once. All of them settle on the\n  confirmed remote exit. An aborted `exec` resolves with the signalled exit\n  code.\n- **Cancellation:** if create fails or times out after Railway minted the\n  sandbox, or the caller aborts while it boots, the provider tries to destroy\n  it. Both are best effort.\n- **Ports:** Railway publishes HTTP domains at create time only. Declare them\n  with `ports` (at most 10), which requires `networkIsolation: 'PRIVATE'`;\n  `ports.connect(port)` returns `https://<domain>` for a declared port and\n  rejects any other. Without `ports`, the `ports` capability is `false`. The\n  domain is public: it carries no preview token.\n- **Network:** `networkPolicy` is `false`. Both modes keep public internet\n  egress, so `policy.capabilities.network: 'deny'` is rejected at create\n  instead of being dropped.\n- **Paths:** the portable root `/workspace` is a real directory created at\n  boot. Override with `workdir`. `fs.remove` is recursive (`rm -rf`) and\n  `fs.lstat` is a GNU `stat` probe that does not follow symlinks.\n- **Bridge:** like the other cloud providers, it is a remote VM, so bridged\n  tools need the tunnel in local dev (see [tools](./tools)).\n\n## Capabilities\n\nProviders declare what they support via `capabilities()`. The flags are:\n\n| Capability | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| `fs` | Read/write the sandbox filesystem. |\n| `exec` | Run commands. |\n| `env` | Inject environment variables. |\n| `ports` | Expose/forward ports (preview URLs). |\n| `backgroundProcesses` | Keep long-running processes alive between calls. |\n| `writableStdin` | A spawned process exposes a writable host→process stdin. `true` for local-process, Docker container, Daytona, Upstash Box, E2B, boxd, and Railway. `false` for Docker Sandboxes (`sbx`), Vercel, Sprites, Blaxel, and Cloudflare. When `false`, stdin-fed harnesses write the prompt to a file and redirect it in the shell. |\n| `killableProcesses` | A spawned process can be forcibly stopped via `SpawnHandle.kill()` **and** aborted mid-flight via the `signal` passed to `spawn`. |\n| `snapshots` | Capture and restore point-in-time snapshots. |\n| `networkPolicy` | Enforce network allow/deny rules. |\n| `durableFilesystem` | Disk that survives across resumes. |\n| `fork` | Branch a sandbox from an existing one. |\n\nCode that uses an **optional** capability checks the flag first and degrades\ngracefully. For example, bootstrap only snapshots when `snapshots` is supported,\nso `localProcessSandbox` simply skips the step. Calling an unsupported optional\nmethod directly (instead of checking the flag) throws an\n`UnsupportedCapabilityError`:\n\n```ts\nimport { localProcessSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-local-process'\n\nconst provider = localProcessSandbox()\nconst caps = provider.capabilities()\n\nif (caps.snapshots) {\n  // safe to take a snapshot\n} else {\n  // degrade gracefully, local-process has no snapshots\n}\n```\n\nUse the flags to write provider-agnostic code: branch on the capability rather\nthan the concrete provider, and your sandbox definition keeps working when you\nswap one provider for another.\n\n### `killableProcesses` across the bundled providers\n\nThis flag is **measured, not asserted**. A wrong `true` hands the journal reader\nan unstoppable `tail -f` and leaks a process per run, so a provider only declares\nit once killing has been observed to work against a real sandbox. Two of these\ndeclarations were once `true` on reasoning alone and both turned out to be false\nwhen probed (Docker's stream destroy left the container-side process in `ps`;\nlocal-process's `sh -c` did not `exec`, so killing the shell left the command\nalive). Anything that cannot be measured yet stays `false`, because `poll` is\nmerely slower while a wrong `follow` is a leak.\n\n| Provider | `killableProcesses` | Why |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Local process | `true` | **Measured.** Kills the process GROUP, not the wrapper: `detached` spawn plus `process.kill(-pid, signal)` on POSIX (killing only the `sh` left the command running, dash does not reliably `exec`); on Windows `taskkill /T` plus a verified sweep, see [Windows teardown](#windows-process-teardown-logger). |\n| Docker | `true` | **Measured.** Signals the process INSIDE the container by the pid the wrapper recorded for itself, process group first and escalating to `KILL`. Destroying the hijacked exec stream is *not* sufficient: it only detaches the client. |\n| Docker Sandboxes (`sbx`) | `false` | Unmeasured. Live tests named-skip until `sbx login`. The handle records a pid in the VM, but `killableProcesses` stays `false` until that kill is observed. |\n| Daytona | `false` | `kill()` only aborts the client-side poll loop and does not await any termination; the `deleteSession` that might terminate the command runs later from the pump's teardown, is failure-swallowed, and is documented as cleanup for a *completed* session. Unmeasured, needs `DAYTONA_API_KEY`. |\n| Vercel | `false` | The abort signal reaches only the HTTP request that STARTS a detached command, so the old `kill()` was a no-op. It now issues the SDK's server-side `Command.kill`, but whether that reaches a forked child (the follow command is a multi-statement shell, so `tail -f` is always a child) is unmeasured, needs Vercel credentials. |\n| Sprites | `true` (unverified) | Not a client-side detach: `kill()` issues a real server-side `POST /exec/<sessionId>/kill` before closing the socket. What that endpoint signals (process group or pid) is undocumented and unmeasured; needs `SPRITES_API_KEY`. |\n| Upstash Box | `true` | **Measured.** `kill()` sends an allowlisted signal (`TERM`/`KILL`/`INT`/`HUP`) that the box agent delivers to the process TREE server-side, so a forked child is signalled too. Verified against production: a spawned `sleep 5 && touch <marker>` was killed and the marker never appeared. Needs `UPSTASH_BOX_API_KEY`. |\n| Blaxel | `true` | The provider reaps supervisor and child process groups, then waits for the remote reaper to finish. Credential-gated journal conformance verifies that cancellation leaves no active follower process. |\n| E2B | `true` | **Measured.** The SDK's own kill is a SIGKILL to the shell pid, and a backgrounded `( … ) & wait` child survived it. Every command therefore runs as a `setsid` group leader and `kill()` runs `kill -KILL -- -<pid>` inside the sandbox. The shared journal conformance kill case passes against a real sandbox. Needs `E2B_API_KEY`. |\n| Cloudflare | `false` | `kill()` is a no-op, and the caller's `AbortSignal` reaches neither `exec` nor `spawn`, because Workers RPC cannot serialize one. |\n| boxd | `true` | **Measured.** The spawn wrapper runs under `setsid`, so the pid it records leads its own process group. `kill()` runs a shell inside the machine that signals that group, escalates to `KILL`, and checks with `kill -0`. Closing the stream alone is not a kill: the process survived it. Verified against production: a spawned `sleep 5 && touch <marker>` was killed and the marker never appeared. Needs `BOXD_API_KEY`. |\n| Railway | `true` | **Measured.** `kill()` and an aborted `exec` send `TERM` to the command's process group and escalate to `KILL` after 2 s. An aborted `spawn` sends `KILL` at once. Each settles only when the remote exit is confirmed. Verified against production: the shared journal conformance kill case passes, and spawned `sleep 5 && touch <marker>` commands (including a backgrounded child) were killed before the marker appeared, for `kill()`, an aborted `exec`, and an aborted `spawn`. Needs `RAILWAY_API_TOKEN` or `RAILWAY_TOKEN` + `RAILWAY_ENVIRONMENT_ID`. |\n\nEach of the remote providers registers the shared journal conformance suite, so\nthe claim is falsifiable rather than asserted: with credentials present the suite\nruns against a real sandbox, and without them it reports a **named skip** carrying\nthe reason instead of a silent pass. Cloudflare's gate is the runtime rather than\ncredentials, its provider can only create a sandbox through a `Sandbox` Durable\nObject binding, which no Node test process has, so its registration is a named\nskip saying exactly that, until a Workers-runtime harness can measure it.\n\nThis flag is required on every provider, including a bring-your-own one. A\nprovider that omitted it would be treated as killable, which is the dangerous\ndefault: a follower process started there could never be reclaimed, and it would\nkeep running inside the sandbox for as long as the sandbox lives.\n\nIt is the flag the [run journal](./journal) reads to decide how to tail a run's\noutput: a killable provider gets a streaming `tail -f`, and a provider like\nCloudflare gets a loop of bounded reads, each of which terminates on its own.\n\nThe flag also bounds what *cancel* can mean. On a `false` provider there is no\nsignal path to the agent process, so the only cancel that actually stops the\nagent means destroying the sandbox, which is what the cancel path does. See\n[what cancel means on a provider that cannot kill](./takeover#what-cancel-means-on-a-provider-that-cannot-kill).\n"}
